(function (d) { if (typeof module === "object" && module.exports) module.exports = d; if (typeof window !== "undefined") window.QR_FORMATS_DATA = d; })({"version":"2026-10-01","note":"Generated by scripts/qr-formats/build.js. Do not edit by hand; edit scripts/qr-formats/src/*.js and rebuild.","formats":{"qc":{"skill":"qc-compare","label":"Quantitative Comparison","count":60},"ds":{"skill":"ds-format","label":"Data Sufficiency","count":60},"di":{"skill":"data-figs","label":"Data Interpretation (figures)","count":60},"aw":{"skill":"applied-wp","label":"Applied Word Problems","count":40}},"choices":{"qc":["Quantity A is greater","Quantity B is greater","The two quantities are equal","The relationship cannot be determined from the information given"],"ds":["Statement (1) ALONE is sufficient, but statement (2) alone is not sufficient.","Statement (2) ALONE is sufficient, but statement (1) alone is not sufficient.","BOTH statements TOGETHER are sufficient, but NEITHER statement ALONE is sufficient.","EACH statement ALONE is sufficient.","Statements (1) and (2) TOGETHER are NOT sufficient."]},"items":[{"id":"qc-001","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"x < 0","colA":"x³","colB":"x²","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Sign first: an odd power keeps the negative, an even power makes it positive.","why":"Do not calculate, read the signs. x is negative. Cubing a negative number keeps it negative (three negatives multiply to a negative), so x³ < 0. Squaring any nonzero number makes it positive, so x² > 0. A negative number is always less than a positive number, so Quantity B is greater for every allowed x. Quick test: x = -1/2 gives x³ = -1/8 and x² = 1/4.","diag":{"0":"Picturing a big positive x. The given says x is negative, and that flips the cube negative.","3":"Fractions do not rescue this one: for x = -1/2 the cube is still negative and the square still positive."}},{"id":"qc-002","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Five numbers have a mean of 12. A sixth number, 12, is added to the list.","colA":"The mean of the six numbers","colB":"12","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Adding a value equal to the mean never moves the mean.","why":"Turn the mean back into a sum. Five numbers with mean 12 add to 5 × 12 = 60. Adding 12 makes the sum 72 across six numbers, and 72 / 6 = 12. The mean is still exactly 12, so the two quantities are equal. The picture: the mean is the pivot of a seesaw, and a new weight set right on the pivot does not tip it.","diag":{"0":"Adding a number does not automatically raise the mean; it only rises if the new number sits above the old mean.","3":"You do not need the five individual numbers; their sum (60) is all the mean depends on."}},{"id":"qc-003","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A fair coin is flipped 4 times.","colA":"The probability of getting exactly 2 heads","colB":"The probability of getting at least 3 heads","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Count the arrangements: probability = (ways) / 2^n for fair flips.","why":"Every sequence of 4 flips is equally likely, and there are 2^4 = 16 of them. Exactly 2 heads: choose which 2 of the 4 flips are heads, C(4,2) = 6 ways, so the probability is 6/16. At least 3 heads means exactly 3 or exactly 4: C(4,3) + C(4,4) = 4 + 1 = 5 ways, so 5/16. Since 6/16 > 5/16, Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"\"At least\" feels bigger because it covers two outcomes, but 3 heads and 4 heads together are only 5 of the 16 sequences.","2":"Check the counts: exactly 2 heads has 6 arrangements, at least 3 has 5."}},{"id":"qc-004","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"absolute-value","diff":2,"stem":"|x - 3| = 5","colA":"x","colB":"0","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Absolute value equations usually split into two answers; test both before you compare.","why":"An absolute value of 5 means x is 5 units from 3 on the number line, in either direction. So x - 3 = 5 gives x = 8, and x - 3 = -5 gives x = -2. With x = 8, Quantity A is greater; with x = -2, Quantity B is greater. Two allowed values give two different relationships, so it cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"You solved only x - 3 = 5. The negative branch, x = -2, is just as valid."}},{"id":"qc-005","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"0 < x < 1","colA":"x²","colB":"x","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Squaring a number between 0 and 1 makes it smaller: a fraction of a fraction.","why":"Picture x as a piece of a whole, say x = 1/2. Then x² = 1/4, which is half of a half, smaller than 1/2. In general x² = x · x, and multiplying x by a number less than 1 shrinks it. So for every x strictly between 0 and 1, x² < x, and Quantity B is greater. The trap is assuming squaring always makes a number bigger; that is only true for numbers greater than 1.","diag":{"0":"Squaring grows numbers bigger than 1. Here x is a fraction, so squaring shrinks it.","3":"There is no exception inside 0 < x < 1; the endpoints 0 and 1, where they would be equal, are excluded."}},{"id":"qc-006","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"algebra","diff":3,"stem":"x ≠ 0","colA":"x + 1/x","colB":"2","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"When the given allows negatives, test a negative before you trust a pattern.","why":"Test positive values first: x = 1 gives 1 + 1 = 2 (equal), x = 2 gives 2.5 (Quantity A greater). Positive x never drops below 2. Now test a negative: x = -1 gives -1 + (-1) = -2, and Quantity B is greater. Because x can be negative, two different relationships are possible, so it cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"That holds only for positive x. The given allows x = -1, which makes Quantity A equal to -2."}},{"id":"qc-007","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"algebra","diff":1,"stem":"y > 0 and x = 3y","colA":"x - y","colB":"2y","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Substitute the given relationship so both columns speak the same variable.","why":"Replace x with 3y in Quantity A: x - y = 3y - y = 2y. Quantity B is also 2y. The two quantities are the same expression, so they are equal for every allowed y."},{"id":"qc-008","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"percent","diff":1,"stem":"","colA":"40% of 65","colB":"65% of 40","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"a% of b always equals b% of a: both are (a × b) / 100.","why":"Write each as a product over 100. 40% of 65 = (40 × 65) / 100 = 2600 / 100 = 26. 65% of 40 = (65 × 40) / 100 = 26. Multiplication does not care about order, so the quantities are equal. This swap is a real time-saver: 8% of 25 is the same as 25% of 8, which is 2."},{"id":"qc-009","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"A price is increased by 20%, and then the new price is decreased by 20%.","colA":"The final price as a percent of the original price","colB":"100%","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Successive percent changes multiply; they do not cancel.","why":"Use multipliers. Up 20% means × 1.2; down 20% means × 0.8. Final = 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96 of the original, which is 96%. The decrease is taken from a bigger number than the increase was, so it removes more than was added. Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"2":"Plus 20% and minus 20% only cancel if both are taken from the same base. The 20% decrease comes off the larger, increased price."}},{"id":"qc-010","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Data set: 3, 7, 7, 9, 14","colA":"The mean of the data set","colB":"The median of the data set","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A high outlier pulls the mean toward it; the median only cares about the middle.","why":"Median: the values are already in order, and the middle (third) value is 7. Mean: (3 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 14) / 5 = 40 / 5 = 8. The 14 sits far above the rest and drags the mean up past the median. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"The median is 7 and the mean is 40 / 5 = 8. Recount the sum if you got a smaller mean."}},{"id":"qc-011","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"Set S: 4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42. Set T is formed by adding 10 to every value in S.","colA":"The standard deviation of S","colB":"The standard deviation of T","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Adding the same number to every value slides the whole set; spread does not change.","why":"Standard deviation measures how far values sit from their mean. Adding 10 to every value moves every value and the mean by exactly 10, so every distance from the mean is unchanged. The spread is identical, and the quantities are equal. You never need to compute the standard deviation here. (Multiplying every value by a number would scale the spread; adding does not.)","diag":{"1":"Bigger values do not mean bigger spread. Every value moved by the same 10, so the gaps between them did not change."}},{"id":"qc-012","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"","colA":"The standard deviation of 12, 14, 16, 18","colB":"The standard deviation of 2, 6, 10","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Compare spread by distances from the mean, not by the size of the numbers.","why":"Quantity A: mean 15; distances 3, 1, 1, 3; squared 9, 1, 1, 9; average squared distance 20 / 4 = 5. Quantity B: mean 6; distances 4, 0, 4; squared 16, 0, 16; average 32 / 3, about 10.7. The second set is more spread out even though its numbers are smaller, so Quantity B is greater. (The conclusion is the same whether you divide by n or by n - 1.)","diag":{"0":"The first set has larger numbers, but they are packed 2 apart. The second set is spread 4 apart."}},{"id":"qc-013","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"counting","diff":3,"stem":"","colA":"The number of distinct arrangements of the letters in LEVEL","colB":"The number of ways to choose 3 books from a shelf of 6 different books","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Arrangements with repeats: n! divided by (repeat count)! for each repeated letter.","why":"LEVEL has 5 letters with L twice and E twice. Arrangements = 5! / (2! × 2!) = 120 / 4 = 30. Choosing 3 of 6 books where order does not matter: C(6,3) = 6! / (3! × 3!) = 20. Since 30 > 20, Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"If you got 120 or 60 for LEVEL, divide out both repeated letters: 120 / (2 × 2) = 30, which still beats 20."}},{"id":"qc-014","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"counting","diff":2,"stem":"","colA":"The number of ways to award a gold, a silver, and a bronze medal among 7 runners","colB":"The number of 4-person committees that can be formed from 10 people","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Order matters (ranked spots) means permutation; order does not matter (a group) means combination.","why":"Medals are ranked spots, so order matters: 7 × 6 × 5 = 210. A committee is just a group, so order does not matter: C(10,4) = (10 × 9 × 8 × 7) / (4 × 3 × 2 × 1) = 5040 / 24 = 210. The quantities are equal.","diag":{"1":"If you used 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5040 for the committee, you counted order. A committee of the same four people is one committee."}},{"id":"qc-015","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"probability","diff":2,"stem":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled.","colA":"The probability that the sum is 7","colB":"The probability that the sum is 6 or 8","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"For two dice, count ordered pairs out of 36.","why":"There are 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely ordered rolls. Sum 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1) is 6 rolls, so 6/36. Sum 6 has 5 rolls and sum 8 has 5 rolls, so 6 or 8 is 10/36. Seven is the single most likely sum, but two neighboring sums together beat it. Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"0":"7 is the most likely single sum, but the column asks about two sums together: 5 + 5 = 10 ways."}},{"id":"qc-016","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"n is a positive integer.","colA":"2^(n+1)","colB":"2^n + 2^n","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Two of the same power is one more power: 2^n + 2^n = 2 · 2^n = 2^(n+1).","why":"Factor Quantity B: 2^n + 2^n = 2 × 2^n. Multiplying by one more 2 raises the exponent by 1, so 2 × 2^n = 2^(n+1). That is exactly Quantity A, so the quantities are equal for every n. Check with n = 3: 2^4 = 16 and 8 + 8 = 16.","diag":{"1":"Adding powers does not add exponents: 2^n + 2^n is 2^(n+1), not 2^(2n)."}},{"id":"qc-017","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"n is an integer greater than 1.","colA":"3^(2n)","colB":"2^(3n)","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Rewrite both with the same exponent, then compare the bases.","why":"3^(2n) = (3²)^n = 9^n and 2^(3n) = (2³)^n = 8^n. With the same positive exponent n, the bigger base wins: 9^n > 8^n for every n ≥ 2. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"A bigger exponent on the outside (3n vs 2n) is not the whole story; regroup to 9^n vs 8^n."}},{"id":"qc-018","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"n is an integer.","colA":"3^(2n)","colB":"2^(3n)","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Exponent of zero or below flips size comparisons; test n = 0 and a negative n.","why":"Regroup: Quantity A is 9^n and Quantity B is 8^n. For n = 1, 9 > 8, so Quantity A is greater. For n = 0, both equal 1. For n = -1, 1/9 < 1/8, so Quantity B is greater. Because n can be any integer, the relationship cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"True for positive n only. Here n can be 0 (equal) or negative (Quantity B greater)."}},{"id":"qc-019","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"inequalities","diff":2,"stem":"3x - 7 ≥ 11","colA":"x","colB":"6","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Watch the equals bar: \"greater than or equal to\" leaves room for a tie.","why":"Solve like an equation: add 7 to get 3x ≥ 18, then divide by 3 to get x ≥ 6. If x is greater than 6, Quantity A is greater. But x = 6 is allowed too, and then the quantities are equal. Two possible relationships, so it cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"x ≥ 6 includes x = 6 exactly, where the quantities tie."}},{"id":"qc-020","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"inequalities","diff":3,"stem":"-2x + 5 ≥ 13","colA":"x","colB":"-4","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Dividing an inequality by a negative flips the sign.","why":"Subtract 5: -2x ≥ 8. Divide by -2 and flip the sign: x ≤ -4. So x could be -4 (equal) or anything smaller, like -5 (Quantity B greater). Because a tie is possible, the relationship cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"You forgot to flip the inequality when dividing by -2. The solution is x ≤ -4.","1":"Close: x is never greater than -4, but it can equal -4, which makes them equal."}},{"id":"qc-021","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"absolute-value","diff":2,"stem":"|x| < 3","colA":"x²","colB":"9","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"An absolute value bound is a distance from 0: squaring a smaller distance gives a smaller square.","why":"|x| < 3 means x is within 3 units of 0, so -3 < x < 3. Squaring removes the sign, and the square grows with the distance from 0. The largest distance is just under 3, so x² is always just under 9 or smaller. For example x = -2.5 gives 6.25. Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"3":"Negative x does not help Quantity A: (-2.9)² = 8.41, still below 9."}},{"id":"qc-022","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"The ratio of a to b is 3 : 5, and a and b are positive.","colA":"b - a","colB":"a/2","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Turn a ratio into parts: a = 3k and b = 5k for some positive k.","why":"Write a = 3k and b = 5k with k > 0. Quantity A: b - a = 5k - 3k = 2k. Quantity B: a/2 = 3k/2 = 1.5k. For any positive k, 2k > 1.5k, so Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"3":"The actual sizes are unknown, but both columns scale with the same k, so the comparison is fixed."}},{"id":"qc-023","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"algebra","diff":3,"stem":"x and y are positive numbers, and x + y = 10.","colA":"xy","colB":"25","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A fixed sum gives the biggest product when the two numbers are equal; check the tie.","why":"With x + y = 10, the product is largest when x = y = 5, giving exactly 25. Any unequal split gives less, such as 4 × 6 = 24 or 1 × 9 = 9. So Quantity A is either equal to 25 or less than 25. Two relationships are possible, so it cannot be determined. Students who stop at \"the max is 25\" pick Quantity B; the equal case sinks that.","diag":{"1":"xy can reach 25 exactly when x = y = 5, so Quantity B is not always greater.","2":"Only the split 5 and 5 gives 25; any other split gives a smaller product."}},{"id":"qc-024","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"x > y > 0","colA":"x/y","colB":"(x + 1)/(y + 1)","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Adding the same amount to top and bottom pulls a fraction toward 1.","why":"Since x > y, the fraction x/y is greater than 1. Adding 1 to both top and bottom pulls any fraction toward 1, so (x + 1)/(y + 1) is closer to 1 and smaller. Algebra check: compare x(y + 1) with y(x + 1), which is xy + x versus xy + y. Since x > y, the left side is bigger, so x/y > (x + 1)/(y + 1). Example: 4/2 = 2 but 5/3 is about 1.67. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"2":"Adding the same number to top and bottom changes the value unless the fraction already equals 1."}},{"id":"qc-025","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"sequences","diff":2,"stem":"In a sequence, the first term is 1, and each term after the first is 3 more than twice the term before it.","colA":"The fifth term of the sequence","colB":"60","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"For a recursive rule, just walk the terms one at a time and write each one down.","why":"Apply \"double, then add 3\" four times. Term 1 = 1. Term 2 = 2(1) + 3 = 5. Term 3 = 2(5) + 3 = 13. Term 4 = 2(13) + 3 = 29. Term 5 = 2(29) + 3 = 61. Since 61 > 60, Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"Count the terms carefully: the fifth term needs four applications of the rule after the first term."}},{"id":"qc-026","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"","colA":"The number of prime numbers between 20 and 40","colB":"The number of prime numbers between 40 and 60","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"List primes by checking divisibility by 2, 3, 5, and 7 only (enough for numbers under 121).","why":"Between 20 and 40: 23, 29, 31, 37. That is 4 primes (21, 27, 33, 39 are multiples of 3; 25, 35 of 5). Between 40 and 60: 41, 43, 47, 53, 59. That is 5 primes (49 = 7 × 7, 51 = 3 × 17, 57 = 3 × 19). Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"2":"Check 51 and 57 carefully: both are multiples of 3. Even so, 40 to 60 has five primes."}},{"id":"qc-027","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"","colA":"15% of 15% of 4,000","colB":"30% of 300","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Percent of a percent multiplies the decimals: 0.15 × 0.15.","why":"Quantity A: 15% of 4,000 is 600, and 15% of 600 is 90. Quantity B: 30% of 300 is 90. Both are 90, so the quantities are equal. The trap is turning \"15% of 15%\" into 30%.","diag":{"0":"15% of 15% is 2.25%, not 30%. 2.25% of 4,000 is 90."}},{"id":"qc-028","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"x is a negative number.","colA":"-x","colB":"x²","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"For a negative x, -x is positive; then the 0 to 1 versus above 1 split decides squares.","why":"Both quantities are positive, so compare sizes. If x = -1/2: -x = 1/2 and x² = 1/4, so Quantity A is greater. If x = -2: -x = 2 and x² = 4, so Quantity B is greater. (At x = -1 they tie.) The relationship depends on x, so it cannot be determined.","diag":{"1":"Squares win only when the size of x is above 1. Try x = -1/2."}},{"id":"qc-029","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Class X has 20 students with a mean score of 80. Class Y has 30 students with a mean score of 90.","colA":"The mean score of all 50 students","colB":"85","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A combined mean leans toward the bigger group.","why":"Total points: 20 × 80 = 1,600 and 30 × 90 = 2,700, for 4,300 points across 50 students. Combined mean = 4,300 / 50 = 86. 85 would be right only if the classes were the same size; the larger class (mean 90) pulls the combined mean up. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"2":"85 is the simple average of 80 and 90, which ignores that Class Y has more students."}},{"id":"qc-030","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Seven consecutive integers have a sum of 91.","colA":"The median of the seven integers","colB":"The mean of the seven integers","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Evenly spaced lists are symmetric: mean = median.","why":"Consecutive integers are evenly spaced, so the list is balanced around its middle value. That makes the mean equal the median. Here the mean is 91 / 7 = 13, and the list is 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, whose middle value is also 13. The quantities are equal."},{"id":"qc-031","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"functions","diff":2,"stem":"f(x) = x² - 4x","colA":"f(5)","colB":"f(-1)","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Plug in carefully, and wrap negative inputs in parentheses.","why":"f(5) = 25 - 20 = 5. f(-1) = (-1)² - 4(-1) = 1 + 4 = 5. The quantities are equal. (A graph explains why: this parabola is symmetric about x = 2, and 5 and -1 are both 3 units from 2.)","diag":{"0":"For f(-1), (-1)² is +1 and -4(-1) is +4, so f(-1) = 5, not -3."}},{"id":"qc-032","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"functions","diff":2,"stem":"f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x²","colA":"f(g(2))","colB":"g(f(2))","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Composite functions work inside out: evaluate the inner function first.","why":"f(g(2)): first g(2) = 4, then f(4) = 2(4) + 3 = 11. g(f(2)): first f(2) = 7, then g(7) = 49. Quantity B is greater. Order matters in composition.","diag":{"2":"Composition is not commutative; f(g(2)) = 11 while g(f(2)) = 49."}},{"id":"qc-033","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"graphical","diff":2,"stem":"Line L passes through the points (2, 5) and (6, 17).","colA":"The slope of line L","colB":"The y-intercept of line L","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Slope is rise over run; then back up from a known point to x = 0 for the intercept.","why":"Slope = (17 - 5) / (6 - 2) = 12 / 4 = 3. To find the y-intercept, start at (2, 5) and move left 2 units to x = 0; the line drops 3 for each unit, so y = 5 - 6 = -1. Slope 3 is greater than intercept -1, so Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"Check the intercept: y = 3x + b through (2, 5) gives 5 = 6 + b, so b = -1."}},{"id":"qc-034","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"graphical","diff":2,"stem":"Line M has the equation 3x - 2y = 12.","colA":"The x-intercept of line M","colB":"The absolute value of the y-intercept of line M","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Intercepts: set the other variable to 0.","why":"x-intercept: set y = 0, so 3x = 12 and x = 4. y-intercept: set x = 0, so -2y = 12 and y = -6. Its absolute value is 6. Since 6 > 4, Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"0":"The y-intercept is -6, but the column asks for its absolute value, 6."}},{"id":"qc-035","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A drawer holds 5 black socks and 3 white socks. Two socks are pulled out at random, without replacement.","colA":"The probability that the two socks match in color","colB":"The probability that the two socks are different colors","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Without replacement, count pairs with combinations: C(group, 2) over C(total, 2).","why":"Total pairs: C(8,2) = 28. Matching: two black, C(5,2) = 10, or two white, C(3,2) = 3, for 13 pairs. Different: 5 × 3 = 15 pairs. Check: 13 + 15 = 28. So the matching probability is 13/28 and the different probability is 15/28. Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"0":"Black is the majority, but a match needs two of the same, and there are more mixed pairs (15) than matching pairs (13)."}},{"id":"qc-036","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A fair coin is flipped.","colA":"The probability of exactly 3 heads in 6 flips","colB":"The probability of exactly 2 heads in 4 flips","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Exactly k heads in n flips = C(n, k) / 2^n; do not assume \"half heads\" is equally likely at every n.","why":"Exactly 3 of 6: C(6,3) / 2^6 = 20/64. Exactly 2 of 4: C(4,2) / 2^4 = 6/16 = 24/64. Getting exactly half heads gets less likely as the number of flips grows, because the outcomes spread over more possible counts. Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"2":"Both are \"exactly half heads,\" but 20/64 is less than 24/64."}},{"id":"qc-037","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"x² = 16 and y³ = -27","colA":"x","colB":"y","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Even powers hide the sign; odd powers keep it.","why":"y³ = -27 has exactly one real solution, y = -3, because a cube keeps the sign. x² = 16 has two: x = 4 or x = -4. If x = 4, Quantity A is greater (4 > -3). If x = -4, Quantity B is greater (-4 < -3). It cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"x could also be -4, which is less than -3."}},{"id":"qc-038","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"x³ = 64 and y² = 9","colA":"x","colB":"y","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Solve each power for every root it allows, then compare against all of them.","why":"x³ = 64 gives exactly x = 4. y² = 9 gives y = 3 or y = -3. Compare 4 with both: 4 > 3 and 4 > -3. Quantity A is greater in every case, so the answer is fixed even though y has two values.","diag":{"3":"y has two values, but x beats both of them, so the comparison never changes."}},{"id":"qc-039","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"equations","diff":2,"stem":"a + b = 9 and a - b = 3","colA":"a²","colB":"b³","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Two linear equations, two unknowns: add them to knock out a variable.","why":"Add the equations: 2a = 12, so a = 6. Then b = 9 - 6 = 3. Quantity A: 6² = 36. Quantity B: 3³ = 27. Quantity A is greater."},{"id":"qc-040","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":4,"stem":"p and q are positive integers, and p/q = 0.75.","colA":"q - p","colB":"1","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A ratio fixes the shape, not the size: p = 3k and q = 4k for any positive integer k.","why":"0.75 = 3/4, so p = 3k and q = 4k for some positive integer k. Then q - p = k. With k = 1 (p = 3, q = 4), q - p = 1 and the quantities are equal. With k = 2 (p = 6, q = 8), q - p = 2 and Quantity A is greater. It cannot be determined.","diag":{"2":"That assumes p = 3 and q = 4, the smallest pair. 6/8 is also 0.75."}},{"id":"qc-041","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"","colA":"(2^10)(5^8)","colB":"10^9","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Pair each 2 with a 5 to make tens, then compare what is left over.","why":"Match eight 2s with the eight 5s: (2^10)(5^8) = (2^2)(2^8 × 5^8) = 4 × 10^8. Quantity B is 10^9 = 10 × 10^8. Since 4 × 10^8 < 10 × 10^8, Quantity B is greater.","diag":{"0":"Only eight 2s pair with the eight 5s; the extra 2^2 = 4 multiplies 10^8, giving 400,000,000."}},{"id":"qc-042","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"","colA":"(-0.5)³","colB":"(-0.5)^4","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Sign before size: an odd power of a negative is negative, an even power is positive.","why":"(-0.5)³ has three negative factors, so it is negative: -0.125. (-0.5)^4 has four, so it is positive: 0.0625. Any positive number beats any negative number, so Quantity B is greater, even though 0.125 is bigger than 0.0625 in size.","diag":{"0":"You compared sizes and ignored the sign. -0.125 is less than 0.0625."}},{"id":"qc-043","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"","colA":"(3.2 × 10^5)(5 × 10^-3)","colB":"1.6 × 10^3","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Scientific notation: multiply the fronts, add the exponents, then tidy up.","why":"Fronts: 3.2 × 5 = 16. Powers: 10^5 × 10^-3 = 10^2. So Quantity A = 16 × 10^2 = 1,600. Quantity B = 1.6 × 10^3 = 1,600. They are equal.","diag":{"0":"16 × 10^2 looks bigger than 1.6 × 10^3, but both are 1,600."}},{"id":"qc-044","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"x > 0","colA":"x% of 50","colB":"50% of x","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Swap trick again: x% of 50 = (x × 50) / 100 = 50% of x.","why":"x% of 50 = (x / 100) × 50 = x / 2. 50% of x = 0.5x = x / 2. Same expression, so they are equal for every positive x."},{"id":"qc-045","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":1,"stem":"The ratio of boys to girls in a class of 36 students is 4 : 5.","colA":"The number of girls in the class","colB":"20","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Ratio to counts: add the parts, divide the total, multiply back out.","why":"4 + 5 = 9 parts. 36 / 9 = 4 students per part. Girls = 5 parts × 4 = 20. The quantities are equal."},{"id":"qc-046","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"inequalities","diff":3,"stem":"x < y and y ≠ 0","colA":"x/y","colB":"1","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Dividing by an unknown sign is risky; test a negative divisor.","why":"If y is positive, dividing x < y by y keeps the direction: x/y < 1, so Quantity B is greater (for example x = 1, y = 2 gives 0.5). If y is negative, the direction flips: x = -3, y = -1 gives x/y = 3, and Quantity A is greater. It cannot be determined.","diag":{"1":"That assumes y is positive. With x = -3 and y = -1, x/y = 3."}},{"id":"qc-047","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"number-properties","diff":3,"stem":"k is an odd integer.","colA":"The remainder when k² is divided by 4","colB":"1","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Write odd as 2m + 1 and expand; the pattern shows up in one line.","why":"Any odd integer is k = 2m + 1. Then k² = 4m² + 4m + 1 = 4(m² + m) + 1, which is a multiple of 4 plus 1. So the remainder is always 1. Check: 3² = 9 = 2 × 4 + 1; 5² = 25 = 6 × 4 + 1. The quantities are equal."},{"id":"qc-048","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"number-properties","diff":3,"stem":"n is a positive integer.","colA":"The remainder when n² + n is divided by 2","colB":"0","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Factor first: n² + n = n(n + 1), two consecutive integers, so one of them is even.","why":"n² + n = n(n + 1). Of any two consecutive integers, one is even, so their product is even. The remainder on division by 2 is always 0, and the quantities are equal."},{"id":"qc-049","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"Data set: 12, 15, 18, 20, 20, 22, 25, 30, 41. The largest value is then increased by 40.","colA":"The increase in the mean","colB":"The increase in the median","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Changing an extreme value moves the mean but not the median.","why":"The largest value goes from 41 to 81. The sum rises by 40 over 9 values, so the mean rises by 40 / 9, about 4.4. The median is the 5th value in order, which is still 20, so the median rises by 0. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"2":"The mean feels every value, including the one that changed. Only the median ignores it."}},{"id":"qc-050","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"algebra","diff":4,"stem":"a and b are positive, and a ≠ b.","colA":"a/b + b/a","colB":"2","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A number plus its reciprocal is at least 2 for positives, and exactly 2 only at 1.","why":"Let t = a/b, which is positive and not 1 because a ≠ b. Quantity A is t + 1/t. Rewrite t + 1/t - 2 = (t² - 2t + 1)/t = (t - 1)²/t. The top is a square, positive since t ≠ 1, and the bottom is positive, so t + 1/t > 2. Example: a = 1, b = 2 gives 0.5 + 2 = 2.5. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"3":"The tie at 2 needs a = b, which the given rules out."}},{"id":"qc-051","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"equations","diff":2,"stem":"x² - 5x + 6 = 0","colA":"x","colB":"2.5","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Factor the quadratic and keep both roots before comparing.","why":"x² - 5x + 6 = (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3. Against 2.5: x = 2 makes Quantity B greater, and x = 3 makes Quantity A greater. It cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"x = 3 is a root, but so is x = 2, which is less than 2.5."}},{"id":"qc-052","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"inequalities","diff":4,"stem":"x² - 5x + 6 ≤ 0","colA":"x","colB":"2","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A quadratic at or below zero lives between its roots, endpoints included.","why":"The quadratic factors as (x - 2)(x - 3), which is negative only between the roots and zero at the roots. So 2 ≤ x ≤ 3. At x = 2 the quantities are equal; for any x above 2 (up to 3), Quantity A is greater. Two relationships, so it cannot be determined.","diag":{"0":"x = 2 itself satisfies the inequality, giving a tie."}},{"id":"qc-053","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"2^x = 16 and y² = 16","colA":"x","colB":"y","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Solve each condition for all of its values before comparing.","why":"2^x = 16 means x = 4 (2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16). y² = 16 means y = 4 or y = -4. With y = 4 the quantities are equal; with y = -4, Quantity A is greater. It cannot be determined.","diag":{"2":"y could also be -4."}},{"id":"qc-054","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"A population grows by 10% each year for 2 years.","colA":"The total percent increase over the 2 years","colB":"20%","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Growth compounds: the second 10% is taken from a bigger base.","why":"Multiply: 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.21, so the population is 121% of where it started, a 21% total increase. The second year adds 10% of the already-grown number, which is a little more than 10% of the original. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"2":"Adding 10% + 10% ignores compounding. The total is 21%."}},{"id":"qc-055","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"interest","diff":4,"stem":"An account starts with 1,000 dollars and no withdrawals or deposits are made.","colA":"Interest earned in 3 years at 10% per year, compounded annually","colB":"Interest earned in 3 years at 11% per year, simple interest","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Compound interest earns interest on interest; a slightly higher simple rate may not catch it.","why":"Compound: 1,000 × 1.1³ = 1,000 × 1.331 = 1,331, so the interest is 331 dollars. Simple: 1,000 × 0.11 × 3 = 330 dollars. Quantity A is greater by 1 dollar. Compounding at 10% adds 0.1 + 0.11 + 0.121 = 0.331 of the start, edging out 3 × 0.11 = 0.33.","diag":{"1":"The higher rate is not enough: compounding at 10% gives 331 dollars of interest, simple at 11% gives 330."}},{"id":"qc-056","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"x is a positive integer.","colA":"x/(x + 1)","colB":"(x + 1)/(x + 2)","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Fractions of the form n/(n + 1) climb toward 1 as n grows.","why":"Each column is a number over one more than itself, and Quantity B uses the bigger number. Think 1/2, 2/3, 3/4: they climb toward 1. Algebra check: cross-multiply x(x + 2) = x² + 2x versus (x + 1)² = x² + 2x + 1. The right side is always 1 bigger, so Quantity B is greater."},{"id":"qc-057","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"counting","diff":3,"stem":"","colA":"The number of three-digit positive integers whose digits are all odd","colB":"The number of three-digit positive integers that are multiples of 8","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Counting with digit rules: slots times choices. Counting multiples: floor(top/k) - floor(below/k).","why":"All digits odd: each of the three digits can be 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9, so 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. Multiples of 8 from 100 to 999: there are floor(999/8) = 124 multiples up to 999, minus floor(99/8) = 12 below 100, which is 112. Quantity A is greater.","diag":{"1":"If you got 900/8 = 112.5 and rounded up, list the ends: 104 is the first and 992 the last, giving (992 - 104)/8 + 1 = 112."}},{"id":"qc-058","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"number-properties","diff":3,"stem":"m and n are consecutive integers, and m < n.","colA":"m²","colB":"n²","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Bigger number does not mean bigger square once negatives are allowed.","why":"Since n = m + 1, compare m² with (m + 1)². For positive pairs, such as 2 and 3, n² is larger, so Quantity B is greater. For negative pairs, such as -3 and -2, m² = 9 and n² = 4, so Quantity A is greater. It cannot be determined.","diag":{"1":"Only for m ≥ 0. With m = -3 and n = -2, m² is larger."}},{"id":"qc-059","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"ratios","diff":4,"stem":"x and y are positive, and x/y = 3/4.","colA":"(x + 3)/(y + 4)","colB":"3/4","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Adding numbers in the same ratio keeps the ratio: (3k + 3)/(4k + 4) = 3/4.","why":"Write x = 3k and y = 4k with k > 0. Then (x + 3)/(y + 4) = (3k + 3)/(4k + 4) = 3(k + 1) / 4(k + 1) = 3/4. Adding 3 and 4, which are in the same 3 : 4 ratio, does not change the fraction. The quantities are equal.","diag":{"1":"Adding to top and bottom only moves a fraction when the added amounts are in a different ratio."}},{"id":"qc-060","format":"qc","skill":"qc-compare","topic":"statistics","diff":1,"stem":"Test scores: 70, 80, 80, 90, 100","colA":"The mode of the scores","colB":"The mean of the scores","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Mode is the most frequent value; mean is the sum over the count.","why":"The mode is 80 because it appears twice. The mean is (70 + 80 + 80 + 90 + 100) / 5 = 420 / 5 = 84. Quantity B is greater."},{"id":"ds-001","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":2,"stem":"What is the value of the integer x?","s1":"x² = 49","s2":"x < 0","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"A squared variable hides two signs; look for the statement that picks one.","why":"Statement (1): x² = 49 allows x = 7 or x = -7. Two values, not sufficient. Statement (2): x < 0 allows any negative integer. Not sufficient. Together: the only value with x² = 49 that is negative is x = -7. One value, so both statements together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"0":"x² = 49 has two roots, 7 and -7. That is two answers, so (1) alone is not enough."}},{"id":"ds-002","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"Is the integer n even?","s1":"3n is even.","s2":"n + 4 is even.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"For a yes/no question, sufficient means the answer is always yes or always no.","why":"Statement (1): 3 is odd, and odd × n is even only when n itself is even. So the answer is always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): adding 4 (an even number) does not change whether n is even or odd, so n + 4 even means n is even. Always yes. Sufficient. Each statement alone answers the question.","diag":{"2":"Each statement already forces n to be even by itself; you do not need to combine them."}},{"id":"ds-003","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":3,"stem":"What is the value of x + y?","s1":"3x + 3y = 21","s2":"x - y = 1","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"You only need the value asked for, not each variable: factor and look for the asked expression.","why":"Statement (1): factor out the 3 to get 3(x + y) = 21, so x + y = 7. You never learn x or y separately, and you do not need to. Sufficient. Statement (2): x - y = 1 allows x = 1, y = 0 (sum 1) and x = 2, y = 1 (sum 3). Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"2":"Combining would let you solve for x and y, but the question only asks for x + y, which (1) gives directly.","4":"One equation with two unknowns can still fix a combination like x + y."}},{"id":"ds-004","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"n is a positive integer. What is the value of n?","s1":"n² < 10","s2":"n is the only prime factor of 27.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Sufficient means exactly one possible answer; list the candidates each statement allows.","why":"Statement (1): n² < 10 allows n = 1, 2, or 3. Three values, not sufficient. Statement (2): 27 = 3 × 3 × 3, so its only prime factor is 3, and n = 3. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not.","diag":{"2":"Statement (2) already names a single value, so you do not need statement (1)."}},{"id":"ds-005","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"A bag contains only red and blue marbles. How many red marbles are in the bag?","s1":"The ratio of red marbles to blue marbles is 2 : 3.","s2":"Red marbles make up 40% of the marbles in the bag.","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Two statements that say the same thing in different words count as one statement.","why":"Statement (1): red : blue = 2 : 3 gives the shape but not the size; 2 red and 3 blue fits, so does 20 red and 30 blue. Not sufficient. Statement (2): red is 40% of the bag, which is 2 parts out of 5, the exact same ratio as statement (1). Not sufficient. Together you still only know the ratio, never a count, so the statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Statement (2) is statement (1) in disguise: 2 out of 5 is 40%. Combining them adds no new information."}},{"id":"ds-006","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"A lab colony contains only mice and rats. How many rats are in the colony?","s1":"The ratio of mice to rats is 5 : 3.","s2":"There are 12 more mice than rats.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"A ratio plus one actual count or difference pins the whole picture.","why":"Statement (1): mice = 5k and rats = 3k for some k; the size is unknown. Not sufficient. Statement (2): a difference of 12 fits 13 and 1, 20 and 8, and many more. Not sufficient. Together: 5k - 3k = 2k = 12, so k = 6 and there are 3 × 6 = 18 rats. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"4":"The ratio turns the difference into a number of parts: 2 parts = 12, so 1 part = 6."}},{"id":"ds-007","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":2,"stem":"Is x > 5?","s1":"x - 3 > 4","s2":"x² > 25","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Solve each statement into a range, then ask: is the whole range on one side of the question?","why":"Statement (1): x > 7, and every number above 7 is above 5. The answer is always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): x² > 25 means x > 5 or x < -5. x = 6 says yes; x = -6 says no. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"3":"x² > 25 also allows x = -6, which is not greater than 5."}},{"id":"ds-008","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"k is an integer. Is k divisible by 6?","s1":"k is divisible by 3.","s2":"k is divisible by 12.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Divisible by a bigger multiple means divisible by every factor of it.","why":"Statement (1): 3 and 9 are both divisible by 3, but 6 is a multiple of 6 and 9 is not. Not sufficient. Statement (2): 12 = 6 × 2, so any multiple of 12 is a multiple of 6. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not.","diag":{"0":"Divisible by 3 is only half of divisible by 6; k also has to be even. Try k = 9."}},{"id":"ds-009","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"k is an integer. Is k divisible by 10?","s1":"k is divisible by 5.","s2":"k is even.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"To be divisible by 10, k needs both prime pieces: a 2 and a 5.","why":"Statement (1): 15 and 20 are both divisible by 5; only 20 is divisible by 10. Not sufficient. Statement (2): 4 and 20 are both even; only 20 is divisible by 10. Not sufficient. Together, k has a factor of 2 and a factor of 5, so it has a factor of 10. Always yes. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-010","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":1,"stem":"What is the value of x?","s1":"4x - 7 = 2x + 5","s2":"x/3 = 2","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"One linear equation in one unknown is always enough on its own.","why":"Statement (1): subtract 2x and add 7 to get 2x = 12, so x = 6. Sufficient. Statement (2): multiply by 3 to get x = 6. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient. On data sufficiency you can stop as soon as you know an equation has one solution; you do not need to finish the arithmetic."},{"id":"ds-011","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":3,"stem":"What is the value of x?","s1":"x² - 7x + 12 = 0","s2":"x > 2","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"A range only helps if it cuts all but one root.","why":"Statement (1): (x - 3)(x - 4) = 0, so x = 3 or x = 4. Not sufficient. Statement (2): any number above 2. Not sufficient. Together: both 3 and 4 are greater than 2, so both survive. Still two values, so the statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Combining only helps if the range removes a root; here 3 and 4 both exceed 2."}},{"id":"ds-012","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":3,"stem":"What is the value of x?","s1":"x² - 7x + 12 = 0","s2":"x² - 9 = 0","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Two quadratics together: keep only the root they share.","why":"Statement (1): (x - 3)(x - 4) = 0 gives x = 3 or 4. Not sufficient. Statement (2): x² = 9 gives x = 3 or -3. Not sufficient. Together, x must be on both lists, and the only shared value is 3. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-013","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"x, y, and z are integers. What is the mean of x, y, and z?","s1":"x + y + z = 24","s2":"x + y = 2z","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A mean only needs the sum and the count; you rarely need the individual values.","why":"Statement (1): the mean is the sum over the count, 24 / 3 = 8. Sufficient, even though x, y, and z themselves are unknown. Statement (2): x = 1, y = 1, z = 1 gives a mean of 1; x = 2, y = 2, z = 2 gives 2. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"2":"Together you could find z = 8, but statement (1) already gives the mean by itself."}},{"id":"ds-014","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"The mean of the integers a, b, and c is 9. What is the value of c?","s1":"a = 3b","s2":"a + b = 20","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Turn a given mean into a sum right away: mean 9 of three numbers means a total of 27.","why":"The stem gives a + b + c = 27. Statement (1): a = 3b gives 4b + c = 27, which still has many solutions (b = 1, c = 23; b = 2, c = 19). Not sufficient. Statement (2): a + b = 20, so c = 27 - 20 = 7. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-015","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":3,"stem":"x and y are nonzero numbers. Is xy > 0?","s1":"x/y > 0","s2":"y = x³","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A product and a quotient share their sign rule: positive means same signs.","why":"Statement (1): a positive quotient means x and y have the same sign, and same signs give a positive product. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): xy = x · x³ = x^4, an even power of a nonzero number, so it is positive. Always yes. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient.","diag":{"0":"Statement (2) works too: y = x³ always has the same sign as x."}},{"id":"ds-016","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":3,"stem":"Is x > y?","s1":"x² > y²","s2":"xy > 0","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Squares erase signs; test a negative pair before trusting a square comparison.","why":"Statement (1): x = 3, y = 2 says yes; x = -3, y = 2 says no. Not sufficient. Statement (2): same signs, but either order is possible. Not sufficient. Together: x = 3, y = 2 works (yes), and x = -3, y = -2 also works (9 > 4 and the product is positive) but -3 < -2 (no). The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Try both negative: x = -3, y = -2 satisfies both statements, and then x < y."}},{"id":"ds-017","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":4,"stem":"Is x > y?","s1":"x² > y²","s2":"x + y > 0","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Factor a difference of squares: x² - y² = (x - y)(x + y).","why":"Statement (1): x = 3, y = 2 says yes; x = -3, y = 2 says no. Not sufficient. Statement (2): x + y > 0 allows x = 5, y = 1 (yes) and x = 1, y = 5 (no). Not sufficient. Together: x² - y² > 0 means (x - y)(x + y) > 0. Since x + y is positive, x - y must be positive too, so x > y. Always yes. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"4":"The factoring move connects them: a positive product with one positive factor forces the other factor positive."}},{"id":"ds-018","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Class P has 20 students and class Q has 30 students. What is the mean score of all 50 students on a quiz?","s1":"The mean score is 80 in class P and 90 in class Q.","s2":"The total of all 50 scores is 4,300.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A combined mean needs the combined total; anything that gives the total is enough.","why":"Statement (1): totals are 20 × 80 = 1,600 and 30 × 90 = 2,700, so 4,300 in all, and the mean is 4,300 / 50 = 86. Sufficient. Statement (2): the combined total is given directly, so the mean is 4,300 / 50 = 86. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-019","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Group X has a mean age of 70 and group Y has a mean age of 90. What is the mean age of the two groups combined?","s1":"Group X has 15 people.","s2":"Group Y has twice as many people as group X.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"A weighted average depends only on the ratio of the group sizes, not the sizes themselves.","why":"Statement (1): with 15 in group X, the mean depends on the size of group Y (15 and 15 gives 80; 15 and 45 gives 85). Not sufficient. Statement (2): sizes x and 2x give (70x + 90 · 2x) / 3x = 250x / 3x = 250/3, about 83.3, no matter what x is. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not.","diag":{"2":"The x cancels: only the 1 : 2 ratio matters, so statement (1) is not needed."}},{"id":"ds-020","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":4,"stem":"v, w, x, y, and z are integers with v ≤ w ≤ x ≤ y ≤ z. What is the median of the five integers?","s1":"v + z = 20","s2":"w + y = 20","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"The median of five ordered values is the middle one; check whether the statements ever touch it.","why":"The median is x. Statement (1) is about the two ends and says nothing about x. Not sufficient. Statement (2) traps x between w and y but does not fix it. Not sufficient. Together: 8, 8, 9, 12, 12 and 8, 8, 11, 12, 12 both satisfy v + z = 20 and w + y = 20, with medians 9 and 11. The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Sums of the outside pairs leave the middle value free between w and y."}},{"id":"ds-021","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"rates","diff":2,"stem":"A car traveled from town P to town Q in two legs. What was the car's average speed, in miles per hour, for the whole trip?","s1":"The first leg was 60 miles and took 1.5 hours.","s2":"The second leg was 90 miles and took 1.5 hours.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Average speed = total distance / total time; you need every leg.","why":"Statement (1) gives the first leg only; the second leg could be anything. Not sufficient. Statement (2) gives the second leg only. Not sufficient. Together: total distance 150 miles over 3 hours, so 50 miles per hour. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-022","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":1,"stem":"n is an integer. Is n odd?","s1":"n + 6 is odd.","s2":"2n + 1 is odd.","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A statement that is true for every integer tells you nothing.","why":"Statement (1): adding the even number 6 does not change odd or even, so n is odd. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): 2n is even for every integer, so 2n + 1 is always odd, whether n is odd or even. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"3":"2n + 1 is odd for n = 2 as well as n = 3, so statement (2) cannot tell them apart."}},{"id":"ds-023","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":3,"stem":"p is a positive integer. Is p a prime number?","s1":"p is odd.","s2":"p is a factor of 21, and p > 7.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"A definite \"no\" is just as sufficient as a definite \"yes.\"","why":"Statement (1): 3 is odd and prime, 9 is odd and not prime. Not sufficient. Statement (2): the factors of 21 are 1, 3, 7, 21, and the only one above 7 is 21 = 3 × 7, which is not prime. The answer is always no, and a definite no is sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not.","diag":{"4":"Sufficient does not mean the answer is yes. Statement (2) always gives no, which settles the question."}},{"id":"ds-024","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"work","diff":2,"stem":"Pumps A and B each work at a constant rate. How many hours do they take to fill a tank working together?","s1":"Pump A alone fills the tank in 6 hours.","s2":"Pump B fills the tank twice as fast as pump A.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Together time = 1 / (rate A + rate B); you need both rates as numbers.","why":"Statement (1) gives pump A only. Not sufficient. Statement (2) gives a relationship but no actual rate. Not sufficient. Together: A takes 6 hours, so B takes 3 hours. Rates 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/2 tank per hour, so together they take 2 hours. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-025","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"What was the original price of a jacket?","s1":"After a 25% discount, the jacket sold for 60 dollars.","s2":"A 10% increase on the original price would have made it 88 dollars.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Any single percent equation with one unknown price is enough.","why":"Statement (1): 75% of the price is 60, so the price is 60 / 0.75 = 80 dollars. Sufficient. Statement (2): 110% of the price is 88, so the price is 88 / 1.1 = 80 dollars. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-026","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"A club has seniors, juniors, and other members. What percent of the club's members are seniors?","s1":"There are 12 seniors in the club.","s2":"There are 8 more juniors than seniors.","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"A percent needs a part and a whole; check that the whole is actually pinned down.","why":"Statement (1) gives the part but not the whole. Not sufficient. Statement (2) relates two groups but gives neither the part nor the whole. Not sufficient. Together: 12 seniors and 20 juniors, but the number of other members is unknown, so the total could be 32, 40, or anything larger. The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Seniors plus juniors is not the whole club; the stem says there are other members too."}},{"id":"ds-027","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"n is a nonnegative integer. What is the remainder when n is divided by 6?","s1":"When n is divided by 12, the remainder is 5.","s2":"When n is divided by 3, the remainder is 2.","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Write the remainder statement as n = (divisor)k + r and divide that form by the new divisor.","why":"Statement (1): n = 12k + 5 = 6(2k) + 5, so dividing by 6 always leaves 5. Sufficient. Statement (2): n = 3k + 2 allows n = 2 (remainder 2 on division by 6) and n = 5 (remainder 5). Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"3":"Because 3 is smaller than 6, a remainder on 3 does not decide the remainder on 6. Test n = 2 and n = 5."}},{"id":"ds-028","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":2,"stem":"n is a nonnegative integer. What is the remainder when n is divided by 4?","s1":"When n is divided by 6, the remainder is 3.","s2":"When n is divided by 8, the remainder is 7.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"A remainder on a multiple of 4 decides the remainder on 4; a remainder on 6 does not.","why":"Statement (1): n could be 3 (remainder 3 on division by 4) or 9 (remainder 1). Not sufficient. Statement (2): n = 8k + 7 = 4(2k + 1) + 3, so the remainder on 4 is always 3. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-029","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":4,"stem":"n is a nonnegative integer. What is the remainder when n is divided by 15?","s1":"When n is divided by 3, the remainder is 2.","s2":"When n is divided by 5, the remainder is 4.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Remainders on 3 and on 5 together fix the remainder on 15; list the numbers that fit both.","why":"Statement (1) allows 2, 5, 8, 11, 14 as remainders on 15. Not sufficient. Statement (2) allows 4, 9, 14. Not sufficient. Together: list numbers that leave 4 on division by 5 (4, 9, 14, 19, 24, 29...) and keep those that leave 2 on division by 3: 14, 29, 44. Each is 15k + 14, so the remainder is always 14. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-030","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":1,"stem":"Is x negative?","s1":"x³ < 0","s2":"-5x > 10","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Odd powers keep the sign, and dividing by a negative flips the inequality.","why":"Statement (1): a cube is negative only when the number is negative. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): divide by -5 and flip: x < -2, so x is negative. Always yes. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-031","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"absolute-value","diff":2,"stem":"Is x > 0?","s1":"x² > 4","s2":"|x| > 3","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Squares and absolute values both throw away the sign, so neither can answer a sign question.","why":"Statement (1): x = 3 and x = -3 both satisfy x² > 4. Not sufficient. Statement (2): x = 4 and x = -4 both satisfy |x| > 3. Not sufficient. Together: x = 4 and x = -4 satisfy both. The statements together are not sufficient."},{"id":"ds-032","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"exponents","diff":1,"stem":"n is an integer. What is the value of n?","s1":"2^n = 64","s2":"n² = 36","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"A positive base raised to a power has one exponent for each result; a square has two roots.","why":"Statement (1): 2^6 = 64, and no other exponent gives 64, so n = 6. Sufficient. Statement (2): n = 6 or n = -6. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-033","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"exponents","diff":2,"stem":"x is an integer. What is the value of x?","s1":"x² = 4³","s2":"x³ = 512","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Even power: two roots. Odd power: one root.","why":"Statement (1): x² = 64 gives x = 8 or x = -8. Not sufficient. Statement (2): a cube has a single real root, and 8³ = 512, so x = 8. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-034","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"ratios","diff":1,"stem":"A basket holds 30 pieces of fruit, all apples or oranges. How many apples are in the basket?","s1":"The ratio of apples to oranges is 2 : 3.","s2":"There are 6 more oranges than apples.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"When the stem gives the total, a ratio or a difference alone is enough.","why":"Statement (1): 2 + 3 = 5 parts make 30, so one part is 6 and apples = 12. Sufficient. Statement (2): a + o = 30 and o - a = 6, so 2a = 24 and a = 12. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient, because the stem already supplies the total.","diag":{"2":"The total of 30 is in the stem, so each statement can work with it alone."}},{"id":"ds-035","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":2,"stem":"A theater sold adult tickets for 12 dollars each and child tickets for 7 dollars each. How many adult tickets were sold?","s1":"A total of 50 tickets were sold.","s2":"Ticket sales totaled 470 dollars.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Two unknowns need two independent equations; check whether one alone has several whole-number answers.","why":"Statement (1): a + c = 50 allows any split. Not sufficient. Statement (2): 12a + 7c = 470 has several whole-number solutions, such as a = 24, c = 26 and a = 31, c = 14. Not sufficient. Together: substitute c = 50 - a to get 12a + 350 - 7a = 470, so 5a = 120 and a = 24. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"1":"A money equation can have more than one whole-number solution: 12(31) + 7(14) = 470 too."}},{"id":"ds-036","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"equations","diff":3,"stem":"Pens cost 2 dollars each and notebooks cost 4 dollars each. Maya bought p pens and n notebooks. What is the value of p?","s1":"Maya spent 40 dollars in all.","s2":"p + 2n = 20","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Before combining, check whether the second equation is just the first one rescaled.","why":"Statement (1): 2p + 4n = 40 allows p = 20, n = 0 or p = 10, n = 5. Not sufficient. Statement (2): p + 2n = 20 allows the same pairs. Not sufficient. Together: divide statement (1) by 2 and you get p + 2n = 20, the same equation. One equation, two unknowns, still many solutions. The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Divide 2p + 4n = 40 by 2: it is identical to statement (2), so combining adds nothing."}},{"id":"ds-037","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"absolute-value","diff":3,"stem":"Is |x - 2| < 3?","s1":"0 < x < 4","s2":"x > -1","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Turn the absolute value into a range first: |x - 2| < 3 means -1 < x < 5.","why":"The question asks whether -1 < x < 5. Statement (1): every x between 0 and 4 is inside that range. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): x = 1 is inside (yes) but x = 6 is not (no). Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-038","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":4,"stem":"Is x² > x?","s1":"x > -1","s2":"x < 0","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"x² > x fails only from 0 to 1; locate x relative to 0 and 1.","why":"x² > x is false exactly when 0 ≤ x ≤ 1. Statement (1): x = 2 gives yes, x = 1/2 gives no. Not sufficient. Statement (2): for negative x, x² is positive and x is negative, so x² > x. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not.","diag":{"2":"Statement (2) already settles it: any negative x makes x² positive and bigger than x."}},{"id":"ds-039","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"inequalities","diff":3,"stem":"Is a > b?","s1":"a = 2b","s2":"b > 0","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Doubling only makes a number bigger when the number is positive.","why":"Statement (1): b = 3 gives a = 6 (yes), but b = -3 gives a = -6 (no). Not sufficient. Statement (2) says nothing about a. Not sufficient. Together: a = 2b with b positive means a is b plus another positive b, so a > b. Always yes. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"0":"Doubling a negative makes it smaller: 2(-3) = -6 < -3."}},{"id":"ds-040","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"functions","diff":2,"stem":"f(x) = ax + 5, where a is a constant. What is the value of f(3)?","s1":"f(1) = 9","s2":"f(2) - f(0) = 8","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A function with one unknown constant needs one equation that involves that constant.","why":"f(3) = 3a + 5, so the question is really \"what is a?\" Statement (1): a + 5 = 9, so a = 4 and f(3) = 17. Sufficient. Statement (2): (2a + 5) - 5 = 2a = 8, so a = 4. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-041","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"functions","diff":3,"stem":"f(x) = ax² + b, where a and b are constants. What is the value of f(2)?","s1":"f(0) = 3","s2":"f(-1) = f(1)","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"A statement that is automatically true for the given form carries no information.","why":"f(2) = 4a + b, so you need both constants. Statement (1): f(0) = b = 3, but a is unknown. Not sufficient. Statement (2): f(-1) = a + b and f(1) = a + b, so this is true for every a and b. It tells you nothing. Not sufficient. Together you know only b = 3. The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Statement (2) is true for every function of this form because (-1)² = 1², so it adds nothing."}},{"id":"ds-042","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"functions","diff":3,"stem":"f(x) = ax + b, where a and b are constants. What is the value of a?","s1":"f(0) = 4","s2":"f(5) - f(2) = 12","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Slope comes from a change in output over a change in input; the intercept is irrelevant.","why":"Statement (1): f(0) = b = 4 gives the intercept only. Not sufficient. Statement (2): f(5) - f(2) = (5a + b) - (2a + b) = 3a = 12, so a = 4. The b cancels. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-043","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"sequences","diff":2,"stem":"In an arithmetic sequence, what is the 10th term?","s1":"The 4th term is 13 and the 7th term is 22.","s2":"The common difference is 3.","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"An arithmetic sequence is fixed by any two of its terms.","why":"Statement (1): from the 4th to the 7th term is 3 steps and the value rises by 9, so the difference is 3. The 10th term is 3 more steps past the 7th: 22 + 9 = 31. Sufficient. Statement (2): the difference alone does not anchor the sequence (start at 1 or at 100, same difference). Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-044","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"sequences","diff":2,"stem":"In an arithmetic sequence, what is the 10th term?","s1":"The 4th term is 13.","s2":"The common difference is 3.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"One anchor term plus the step size builds the whole sequence.","why":"Statement (1) gives one term but no step size. Not sufficient. Statement (2) gives the step size but no anchor. Not sufficient. Together: the 10th term is 6 steps after the 4th, so 13 + 6 × 3 = 31. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-045","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"probability","diff":2,"stem":"A box contains only red chips and green chips. If one chip is drawn at random, what is the probability that it is red?","s1":"There are 3 green chips for every 2 red chips.","s2":"The number of green chips is 50% greater than the number of red chips.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"A probability is a ratio, so a ratio alone can be enough.","why":"Statement (1): red : green = 2 : 3, so red is 2 out of every 5 chips, probability 2/5. Sufficient. Statement (2): green = 1.5 × red, which is the same 2 : 3 ratio, so again 2/5. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient. You never need the actual number of chips.","diag":{"4":"Unlike a count, a probability only needs the ratio, and each statement gives it."}},{"id":"ds-046","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A club has n members, some of whom play chess and some of whom play checkers. If a member is chosen at random, what is the probability that the member plays chess?","s1":"12 members play chess.","s2":"8 members do not play checkers.","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Check that each statement is about the group in the question, not a neighboring group.","why":"The probability is (chess players) / n. Statement (1) gives the top but not n. Not sufficient. Statement (2) is about checkers, which says nothing about chess, and it does not give n either. Not sufficient. Together: 12 chess players, but n is still unknown (it could be 20 or 30). The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Statement (2) describes checkers players. It does not fix n or the chess count."}},{"id":"ds-047","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"Is the mean of 4, 7, and x greater than 6?","s1":"x > 8","s2":"x > 6","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Convert \"mean greater than 6\" into \"sum greater than 18,\" then solve for x.","why":"The mean is greater than 6 exactly when 4 + 7 + x > 18, that is, x > 7. Statement (1): x > 8 guarantees x > 7. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): x = 6.5 gives no; x = 10 gives yes. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-048","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"What is the range of the four numbers 3, 9, x, and y?","s1":"x and y are both between 3 and 9, inclusive.","s2":"x = y","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Range = largest minus smallest; ask whether the new values could become an end.","why":"Statement (1): if x and y sit between 3 and 9, then 3 is still the smallest and 9 is still the largest, so the range is 9 - 3 = 6 whatever x and y are. Sufficient. Statement (2): x = y = 5 gives range 6, but x = y = 20 gives range 17. Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not.","diag":{"4":"You do not need x and y themselves, only that they stay inside the existing ends."}},{"id":"ds-049","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What is the median of the four numbers 2, 5, 8, and x?","s1":"x > 2","s2":"x > 8","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"With four values, the median is the average of the middle two; find which two are in the middle.","why":"Statement (1): if x = 3, the middle two are 3 and 5 (median 4); if x = 6, they are 5 and 6 (median 5.5). Not sufficient. Statement (2): if x > 8, the order is 2, 5, 8, x, so the middle two are always 5 and 8 and the median is 6.5. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-050","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What is the mean of x and y?","s1":"x - y = 6","s2":"2x - 2y = 12","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"A difference never tells you a sum; and a doubled equation is the same equation.","why":"The mean is (x + y) / 2, so you need x + y. Statement (1): x = 6, y = 0 (mean 3) and x = 10, y = 4 (mean 7) both work. Not sufficient. Statement (2) is statement (1) multiplied by 2. Not sufficient. Together it is still one equation. The statements together are not sufficient."},{"id":"ds-051","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"algebra","diff":4,"stem":"What is the mean of x and y?","s1":"x - y = 6","s2":"x² - y² = 48","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Spot the difference of squares: x² - y² = (x - y)(x + y).","why":"You need x + y. Statement (1) gives only the difference. Not sufficient. Statement (2): x = 7, y = 1 gives 48 (mean 4), and x = -7, y = -1 also gives 48 (mean -4). Not sufficient. Together: (x - y)(x + y) = 48 and x - y = 6, so 6(x + y) = 48 and x + y = 8. The mean is 4. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is.","diag":{"1":"x² - y² = 48 fits both (7, 1) and (-7, -1), which have different means."}},{"id":"ds-052","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"absolute-value","diff":3,"stem":"Is |x| > 4?","s1":"x² > 20","s2":"x < -5","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"|x| is the distance from 0; compare each statement against \"more than 4 away.\"","why":"Statement (1): x² > 20 means |x| > √20, which is about 4.47, more than 4. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2): any x below -5 is more than 5 away from 0. Always yes. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-053","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"absolute-value","diff":2,"stem":"Is |x + 1| > 3?","s1":"x < 3","s2":"x > 2.5","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Rewrite the question as a range: |x + 1| > 3 means x > 2 or x < -4.","why":"Statement (1): x = 0 gives |1| = 1 (no), and x = -5 gives |-4| = 4 (yes). Not sufficient. Statement (2): every x above 2.5 is above 2, so |x + 1| > 3. Always yes. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-054","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"interest","diff":4,"stem":"A deposit earns simple interest at a constant annual rate, with no withdrawals. What is the annual interest rate, in percent?","s1":"After 4 years the account holds 1,200 dollars.","s2":"The interest earned in the first year was 50 dollars.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"Simple interest: amount in the account = principal + years × (yearly interest). Two unknowns need two facts.","why":"Let P be the deposit and I the yearly interest. Statement (1): P + 4I = 1,200 fits P = 1,000 at 5% and P = 960 at 6.25%. Not sufficient. Statement (2): I = 50 fits P = 1,000 at 5% and P = 500 at 10%. Not sufficient. Together: P + 200 = 1,200, so P = 1,000 and the rate is 50 / 1,000 = 5%. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-055","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"mixture","diff":3,"stem":"A container holds 10 liters of a salt solution. How many liters of pure water must be added to make the solution exactly 20% salt?","s1":"The solution is currently 30% salt.","s2":"The solution currently contains more than 2 liters of salt.","opts":null,"correct":0,"move":"Adding water never changes the amount of salt; fix the salt, then find the new total.","why":"Statement (1): 30% of 10 liters is 3 liters of salt. For that to be 20%, the total must be 3 / 0.2 = 15 liters, so add 5 liters of water. Sufficient. Statement (2): more than 2 liters of salt allows 3 liters (add 5) or 4 liters (add 10). Not sufficient. Statement (1) alone is sufficient; statement (2) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-056","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"work","diff":3,"stem":"Machine M works at a constant rate. How many hours does machine M alone take to fill an order?","s1":"Machine M fills one third of the order in 2 hours.","s2":"Machines M and N together fill the order in 4 hours, and machine N alone takes 12 hours.","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Rates subtract cleanly: rate of M = (together rate) - (rate of N).","why":"Statement (1): one third in 2 hours means the whole order in 6 hours. Sufficient. Statement (2): together rate 1/4, N rate 1/12, so M rate = 1/4 - 1/12 = 3/12 - 1/12 = 1/6, which is 6 hours. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient."},{"id":"ds-057","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"rates","diff":3,"stem":"Car A and car B each drove at a constant speed. Did car A travel farther than car B?","s1":"Car A drove faster than car B.","s2":"Car A drove 60 miles per hour for 3 hours, and car B drove 50 miles per hour for 3.5 hours.","opts":null,"correct":1,"move":"Distance = speed × time; faster is not farther unless the times are known.","why":"Statement (1): faster helps, but car B could have driven much longer. A at 60 for 2 hours (120 miles) loses to B at 50 for 3 hours (150 miles). Not sufficient. Statement (2): A covers 60 × 3 = 180 miles and B covers 50 × 3.5 = 175 miles. Yes. Sufficient. Statement (2) alone is sufficient; statement (1) alone is not."},{"id":"ds-058","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"percent","diff":1,"stem":"Every employee at a company works either full time or part time. How many employees work part time?","s1":"40% of the employees work part time.","s2":"There are 90 full-time employees.","opts":null,"correct":2,"move":"A percent plus one real count unlocks every count.","why":"Statement (1) gives the split but no size. Not sufficient. Statement (2) gives the full-time count but not the part-time count. Not sufficient. Together: full time is 60%, so 0.6 × total = 90, total = 150, and part time = 150 - 90 = 60. Both together are sufficient, but neither alone is."},{"id":"ds-059","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"number-properties","diff":3,"stem":"n is an integer. Is n divisible by 4?","s1":"n is even.","s2":"n is divisible by 6.","opts":null,"correct":4,"move":"Count the factors of 2: divisible by 4 needs two of them.","why":"Statement (1): 2 is even but not divisible by 4; 8 is both. Not sufficient. Statement (2): 6 is not divisible by 4; 12 is. Not sufficient. Together: multiples of 6 are already even, so statement (1) adds nothing, and 6 (no) and 12 (yes) still both fit. The statements together are not sufficient.","diag":{"2":"Even and divisible by 6 guarantees only one factor of 2. n = 6 is not divisible by 4."}},{"id":"ds-060","format":"ds","skill":"ds-format","topic":"exponents","diff":4,"stem":"x is an integer. What is the value of 3^x?","s1":"9^x = 81","s2":"3^(2x) - 2 · 3^x = 63","opts":null,"correct":3,"move":"Spot the hidden quadratic: let u = 3^x, and remember u must be positive.","why":"Statement (1): 9^x = 81 = 9², so x = 2 and 3^x = 9. Sufficient. Statement (2): let u = 3^x. Then u² - 2u - 63 = 0 factors as (u - 9)(u + 7) = 0, so u = 9 or u = -7. A power of 3 is always positive, so u = 9. Sufficient. Each statement alone is sufficient.","diag":{"0":"Statement (2) looks like it has two answers, but 3^x can never be -7."}},{"id":"di-001","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"According to the table, by what percent did total enrollment in the program increase from 2021 to 2023?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Students enrolled in a summer science program","columns":["Year","Biology","Chemistry","Physics"],"rows":[["2021",120,80,50],["2022",150,90,60],["2023",165,72,63]]},"opts":["8%","12.5%","16.7%","18%","20%"],"correct":4,"move":"Percent change = (new - old) / old. The old value is always the denominator.","why":"Add each row first. 2021: 120 + 80 + 50 = 250. 2023: 165 + 72 + 63 = 300. Change = 300 - 250 = 50. Percent change = 50 / 250 = 0.20 = 20%. The trap is dividing by the newer total: 50 / 300 is about 16.7%.","diag":{"2":"You divided the change by the 2023 total. Percent change always divides by the starting value."}},{"id":"di-002","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":3,"stem":"Based on the chart, which month showed the greatest percent increase in questions completed over the previous month?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Practice questions completed per month","categories":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"name":"Questions","values":[20,30,36,48,45,60]}],"yLabel":"Questions"},"opts":["Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"correct":0,"move":"Biggest jump in raw count is not the biggest percent jump: divide each change by its own starting value.","why":"Compute each change over the month before it. February: (30 - 20) / 20 = 50%. March: (36 - 30) / 30 = 20%. April: (48 - 36) / 36 = 33.3%. May: (45 - 48) / 48 is a decrease. June: (60 - 45) / 45 = 33.3%. February wins at 50%, even though June has the largest raw increase (15). A small starting value makes a small jump a big percent.","diag":{"2":"April is 12 / 36 = 33.3%, well below February.","4":"June has the largest raw increase (15), but from a base of 45 that is only 33.3%."}},{"id":"di-003","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"According to the chart, what percent of the surveyed students chose neither flashcards nor practice tests?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Preferred study method of 400 surveyed students","show":"value","slices":[{"label":"Flashcards","value":120},{"label":"Practice tests","value":100},{"label":"Videos","value":80},{"label":"Textbook","value":60},{"label":"Group study","value":40}]},"opts":["30%","35%","40%","45%","55%"],"correct":3,"move":"\"Neither\" means add up everything else, or take the total minus the named groups.","why":"Students who chose neither: 400 - 120 - 100 = 180 (the same as 80 + 60 + 40). As a percent of everyone surveyed: 180 / 400 = 0.45 = 45%. The 55% choice is the share that did pick flashcards or practice tests, the complement of what was asked.","diag":{"0":"Videos and group study alone give 30%. Textbook readers also chose neither flashcards nor practice tests.","4":"That is the share who did choose flashcards or practice tests (220 / 400), the complement of what was asked."}},{"id":"di-004","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":1,"stem":"According to the table, what was the mean number of teas sold per day from Monday through Friday?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Drinks sold at a campus cafe","columns":["Day","Coffee","Tea","Smoothie"],"rows":[["Mon",120,45,30],["Tue",110,50,35],["Wed",130,40,25],["Thu",125,55,40],["Fri",150,60,45]]},"opts":["50","52.5","55","60","62.5"],"correct":0,"move":"Mean = column total / number of rows; add down one column only.","why":"Add the Tea column: 45 + 50 + 40 + 55 + 60 = 250. Divide by the 5 days: 250 / 5 = 50 teas per day. 62.5 comes from dividing by 4, as if one day were missing.","diag":{"3":"That is Friday alone, not the mean of the week.","4":"There are five days in the table, so divide 250 by 5, not 4."}},{"id":"di-005","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"On which day did coffee make up the greatest fraction of all drinks sold that day?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Drinks sold at a campus cafe","columns":["Day","Coffee","Tea","Smoothie"],"rows":[["Mon",120,45,30],["Tue",110,50,35],["Wed",130,40,25],["Thu",125,55,40],["Fri",150,60,45]]},"opts":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri"],"correct":2,"move":"A share of a total needs the total for each row; the biggest count is not always the biggest share.","why":"Find each day's total and the coffee share. Mon: 120 / 195, about 0.615. Tue: 110 / 195, about 0.564. Wed: 130 / 195, about 0.667. Thu: 125 / 220, about 0.568. Fri: 150 / 255, about 0.588. Wednesday has the largest share, even though Friday sold the most coffee.","diag":{"4":"Friday sold the most coffee, but it also sold the most of everything else, so coffee is only about 59% of Friday."}},{"id":"di-006","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"Based on the table, which treatment had the lowest germination rate (seeds germinated divided by seeds planted)?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Seed germination trial","columns":["Treatment","Planted","Germinated"],"rows":[["Control",80,52],["Water",70,49],["Light",60,45],["Heat",50,41],["Shade",40,22]]},"opts":["Control","Water","Light","Heat","Shade"],"correct":4,"move":"Compare rates, not counts: divide each row before you rank.","why":"Rates: Control 52 / 80 = 0.65. Water 49 / 70 = 0.70. Light 45 / 60 = 0.75. Heat 41 / 50 = 0.82. Shade 22 / 40 = 0.55. Shade is lowest. Shade also has the fewest seeds germinated, but that alone would not prove it; Control loses the most seeds (28) yet has a higher rate.","diag":{"0":"Control has the most seeds that failed (28), but it also planted the most. Its rate, 0.65, beats Shade at 0.55."}},{"id":"di-007","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"Across all five treatments combined, what percent of the planted seeds germinated, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Seed germination trial","columns":["Treatment","Planted","Germinated"],"rows":[["Control",80,52],["Water",70,49],["Light",60,45],["Heat",50,41],["Shade",40,22]]},"opts":["67.3%","69.4%","69.7%","71.0%","73.5%"],"correct":2,"move":"An overall rate is total part over total whole; never average the row rates when the rows have different sizes.","why":"Total planted: 80 + 70 + 60 + 50 + 40 = 300. Total germinated: 52 + 49 + 45 + 41 + 22 = 209. Overall rate: 209 / 300 = 0.6967, or 69.7%. Averaging the five row rates (0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.82, 0.55) gives 69.4%, which weights the small Shade group the same as the large Control group.","diag":{"1":"That is the average of the five rates. The groups have different sizes, so pool the counts first."}},{"id":"di-008","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"In how many of the months shown did City B receive more than 1.2 times the rainfall of City A?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Monthly rainfall in two cities (mm)","columns":["Month","City A","City B"],"rows":[["Apr",82,64],["May",95,71],["Jun",60,88],["Jul",45,102],["Aug",38,96]]},"opts":["0","1","2","3","4"],"correct":3,"move":"Turn \"more than 1.2 times\" into a threshold for each row: compute 1.2 × A, then compare.","why":"Compute 1.2 × City A for each month and compare with City B. Apr: 98.4 vs 64, no. May: 114 vs 71, no. Jun: 72 vs 88, yes. Jul: 54 vs 102, yes. Aug: 45.6 vs 96, yes. Three months.","diag":{"2":"Check June: 1.2 × 60 = 72, and City B had 88, which is more."}},{"id":"di-009","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What was the difference between the mean monthly rainfall of City B and the mean monthly rainfall of City A over these five months, in millimeters?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Monthly rainfall in two cities (mm)","columns":["Month","City A","City B"],"rows":[["Apr",82,64],["May",95,71],["Jun",60,88],["Jul",45,102],["Aug",38,96]]},"opts":["12","16.4","20.2","28","57"],"correct":2,"move":"Difference of means = (difference of totals) / count; one subtraction instead of two averages.","why":"City A total: 82 + 95 + 60 + 45 + 38 = 320, mean 64. City B total: 64 + 71 + 88 + 102 + 96 = 421, mean 84.2. Difference: 84.2 - 64 = 20.2 mm. Shortcut: (421 - 320) / 5 = 101 / 5 = 20.2.","diag":{"3":"That is the difference of the medians (88 - 60). The question asks about means."}},{"id":"di-010","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"Which route had the greatest percent increase in riders from 2024 to 2025?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Average weekday bus riders (thousands)","columns":["Route","2024","2025"],"rows":[["Route 1",40,46],["Route 2",25,24],["Route 3",32,40],["Route 4",18,27],["Route 5",50,60]]},"opts":["Route 1","Route 2","Route 3","Route 4","Route 5"],"correct":3,"move":"Percent change rewards a small starting value; divide each change by its own 2024 number.","why":"Route 1: 6 / 40 = 15%. Route 2: -1 / 25 = -4%. Route 3: 8 / 32 = 25%. Route 4: 9 / 18 = 50%. Route 5: 10 / 50 = 20%. Route 4 leads. Route 5 has the largest raw gain (10 thousand), but from the largest base.","diag":{"4":"Route 5 gained the most riders, but 10 on a base of 50 is only 20%."}},{"id":"di-011","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"By what percent did total weekday ridership on the five routes increase from 2024 to 2025, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Average weekday bus riders (thousands)","columns":["Route","2024","2025"],"rows":[["Route 1",40,46],["Route 2",25,24],["Route 3",32,40],["Route 4",18,27],["Route 5",50,60]]},"opts":["14.0%","16.2%","19.4%","21.2%","32.0%"],"correct":2,"move":"For a total, add the columns first, then take one percent change.","why":"Total 2024: 40 + 25 + 32 + 18 + 50 = 165. Total 2025: 46 + 24 + 40 + 27 + 60 = 197. Change: 32. Percent: 32 / 165 = 0.1939, or 19.4%. Averaging the five route percents gives 21.2%, which is wrong because the routes have different sizes. Dividing by the 2025 total gives 16.2%.","diag":{"1":"Divide the change by the starting total, 165, not by 197.","3":"That averages the route percents. Pool the riders, then divide once."}},{"id":"di-012","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":2,"stem":"A customer orders 60 flyers and 3 posters. Using the price table, what is the total cost of the order?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Print shop price per item (dollars)","columns":["Item","1 to 49","50 or more"],"rows":[["Poster",6,4.5],["Flyer",0.5,0.35],["Banner",20,16]],"note":"The price per item depends on how many of that item are ordered."},"opts":["34.5 dollars","39 dollars","43.5 dollars","48 dollars","51 dollars"],"correct":1,"move":"Pick the tier row by row: each item's own quantity decides its price.","why":"Flyers: 60 is in the 50 or more tier, so 60 × 0.35 = 21 dollars. Posters: 3 is in the 1 to 49 tier, so 3 × 6 = 18 dollars. Total: 21 + 18 = 39 dollars. Using the bulk price for the posters too gives 34.5; using the small-order price for the flyers gives 48.","diag":{"0":"Only 3 posters were ordered, so they stay at the 1 to 49 price of 6 dollars.","3":"60 flyers qualify for the 50 or more price, 0.35 each."}},{"id":"di-013","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"According to the table, what percent of the surveyed students study fewer than 15 hours per week?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Weekly study hours of 200 surveyed students","columns":["Hours per week","Students"],"rows":[["0 to 4",30],["5 to 9",70],["10 to 14",60],["15 to 19",30],["20 or more",10]]},"opts":["45%","50%","65%","80%","85%"],"correct":3,"move":"\"Fewer than\" a boundary: add every row that sits completely below it.","why":"Fewer than 15 hours covers the rows 0 to 4, 5 to 9, and 10 to 14: 30 + 70 + 60 = 160 students. As a percent of 200: 160 / 200 = 80%. Stopping one row early (30 + 70 = 100) gives 50%.","diag":{"1":"The 10 to 14 row is also below 15 hours. Include its 60 students.","4":"The 15 to 19 row is not fewer than 15 hours."}},{"id":"di-014","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What is the median of the six participants' Week 2 exercise minutes?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Exercise minutes per week","columns":["Participant","Week 1","Week 2"],"rows":[["P1",150,180],["P2",90,120],["P3",200,170],["P4",60,100],["P5",120,150],["P6",180,210]]},"opts":["150","155","160","170","180"],"correct":2,"move":"Median of an even count: sort, then average the two middle values.","why":"Sort the Week 2 values: 100, 120, 150, 170, 180, 210. With six values, the median is the mean of the 3rd and 4th: (150 + 170) / 2 = 160. The mean, 930 / 6 = 155, is a different measure.","diag":{"1":"That is the mean of Week 2. The median uses only the middle two sorted values.","3":"Sort before picking the middle; 170 is the 4th value, and the median averages the 3rd and 4th."}},{"id":"di-015","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"For how many participants did weekly exercise minutes increase by more than 20% from Week 1 to Week 2?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Exercise minutes per week","columns":["Participant","Week 1","Week 2"],"rows":[["P1",150,180],["P2",90,120],["P3",200,170],["P4",60,100],["P5",120,150],["P6",180,210]]},"opts":["2","3","4","5","6"],"correct":1,"move":"\"More than 20%\" means Week 2 must beat 1.2 × Week 1; exactly 20% does not count.","why":"Compare Week 2 with 1.2 × Week 1. P1: 1.2 × 150 = 180, and Week 2 is exactly 180, so not more than 20%. P2: 108 vs 120, yes. P3: decreased. P4: 72 vs 100, yes. P5: 144 vs 150, yes. P6: 216 vs 210, no. Three participants.","diag":{"2":"P1 rose exactly 20% (150 to 180). The question asks for more than 20%."}},{"id":"di-016","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":2,"stem":"If the pattern in the table continues, what will the concentration be at hour 12, in mg/L?","figure":{"type":"table","title":"Drug concentration in blood after one dose","columns":["Hour","Concentration (mg/L)"],"rows":[["0",80],["2",40],["4",20],["6",10],["8",5]]},"opts":["0","1.25","2.5","3.75","5"],"correct":1,"move":"Spot the rule first: equal time steps with equal ratios means repeated halving, not a straight line.","why":"Each 2 hours the concentration halves: 80, 40, 20, 10, 5. Continue: hour 10 is 2.5, hour 12 is 1.25. A straight-line guess (dropping 5 every 2 hours from hour 6 to 8) would reach 0 too early; halving never reaches 0.","diag":{"0":"Halving shrinks the amount but never drops it to zero.","2":"That is hour 10. One more 2-hour step halves it again."}},{"id":"di-017","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"Which age group had the greatest percent increase in volunteers from 2024 to 2025?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Volunteers by age group","categories":["18-24","25-34","35-44","45-54","55+"],"series":[{"name":"2024","values":[40,55,35,30,20]},{"name":"2025","values":[50,50,42,36,32]}],"yLabel":"Volunteers"},"opts":["18-24","25-34","35-44","45-54","55+"],"correct":4,"move":"Percent change: (2025 - 2024) / 2024 for each pair of bars.","why":"18-24: 10 / 40 = 25%. 25-34: a decrease. 35-44: 7 / 35 = 20%. 45-54: 6 / 30 = 20%. 55+: 12 / 20 = 60%. The 55+ group rose the most in percent and in raw count.","diag":{"0":"18-24 rose by 10 on a base of 40, which is 25%. 55+ rose by 12 on a base of 20."}},{"id":"di-018","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"By what percent did the total number of volunteers increase from 2024 to 2025, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Volunteers by age group","categories":["18-24","25-34","35-44","45-54","55+"],"series":[{"name":"2024","values":[40,55,35,30,20]},{"name":"2025","values":[50,50,42,36,32]}],"yLabel":"Volunteers"},"opts":["10.0%","14.3%","16.7%","20.0%","30.0%"],"correct":2,"move":"Total percent change: sum each year, then (new - old) / old.","why":"2024 total: 40 + 55 + 35 + 30 + 20 = 180. 2025 total: 50 + 50 + 42 + 36 + 32 = 210. Increase: 30. Percent: 30 / 180 = 0.1667, or 16.7%. Dividing by 210 instead gives 14.3%.","diag":{"1":"Use the 2024 total (180) as the base, not 210."}},{"id":"di-019","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What was the household's mean monthly electricity use for the six months shown?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Household electricity use by month","categories":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"name":"kWh","values":[640,580,500,430,410,560]}],"yLabel":"kWh"},"opts":["520 kWh","525 kWh","530 kWh","560 kWh","624 kWh"],"correct":0,"move":"Mean of a bar chart: add every bar, divide by the number of bars.","why":"Sum: 640 + 580 + 500 + 430 + 410 + 560 = 3,120. Six months: 3,120 / 6 = 520 kWh. The median would be (500 + 560) / 2 = 530, and dividing by 5 gives 624.","diag":{"2":"That is the median of the six values, not the mean.","4":"There are six bars, so divide by 6."}},{"id":"di-020","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"The longest average commute shown is what percent longer than the shortest, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Average commute time by town","categories":["Ashford","Belmont","Carver","Dalton","Easton","Fulton"],"series":[{"name":"Minutes","values":[24,31,18,27,35,22]}],"yLabel":"Minutes","orientation":"horizontal"},"opts":["48.6%","51.4%","94.4%","105.6%","194.4%"],"correct":2,"move":"\"Percent longer than X\" divides the difference by X, the thing being compared against.","why":"Longest: Easton, 35 minutes. Shortest: Carver, 18 minutes. Difference: 17. Percent longer than the shortest: 17 / 18 = 0.944, or 94.4%. Dividing by 35 gives 48.6% (wrong base), and 35 / 18 = 194.4% is the ratio, not the increase.","diag":{"0":"The base is the shortest commute (18), because the question compares to it.","4":"35 / 18 says Easton is 194.4% of Carver; subtract 100% to get how much longer."}},{"id":"di-021","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":2,"stem":"In which topic was the improvement from before to after the smallest, in points?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Scores before and after a review course","categories":["Algebra","Stats","Percent","Ratios","Probability"],"series":[{"name":"Before","values":[62,55,58,70,48]},{"name":"After","values":[74,68,70,76,66]}],"yLabel":"Score"},"opts":["Algebra","Stats","Percent","Ratios","Probability"],"correct":3,"move":"Read each pair of bars as a gap; the smallest gap wins, not the shortest bar.","why":"Improvements: Algebra 74 - 62 = 12. Stats 68 - 55 = 13. Percent 70 - 58 = 12. Ratios 76 - 70 = 6. Probability 66 - 48 = 18. Ratios improved least. Probability has the lowest scores but the biggest gain.","diag":{"4":"Probability has the shortest bars, but its gain (18 points) is the largest."}},{"id":"di-022","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What was the mean improvement, in points, across the five topics?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Scores before and after a review course","categories":["Algebra","Stats","Percent","Ratios","Probability"],"series":[{"name":"Before","values":[62,55,58,70,48]},{"name":"After","values":[74,68,70,76,66]}],"yLabel":"Score"},"opts":["6","11.2","12","12.2","13"],"correct":3,"move":"Mean change = (sum of after - sum of before) / count.","why":"Sum after: 74 + 68 + 70 + 76 + 66 = 354. Sum before: 62 + 55 + 58 + 70 + 48 = 293. Difference: 61. Mean improvement: 61 / 5 = 12.2 points. The median improvement is 12, a different measure.","diag":{"2":"That is the median of the improvements (6, 12, 12, 13, 18). The mean is 61 / 5."}},{"id":"di-023","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":1,"stem":"What percent of the week's visitors came on Saturday or Sunday?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Science museum visitors (hundreds)","categories":["Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"name":"Visitors","values":[12,9,11,15,28,25]}],"yLabel":"Hundreds of visitors"},"opts":["43%","47%","50%","53%","56%"],"correct":3,"move":"Part over whole: add the bars you want, then divide by all the bars.","why":"Weekend: 28 + 25 = 53 hundred. Whole week: 12 + 9 + 11 + 15 + 28 + 25 = 100 hundred. 53 / 100 = 53%.","diag":{"1":"That is the weekday share, the complement of what was asked."}},{"id":"di-024","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"The number of science books borrowed was what percent of the number of fiction books borrowed, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Library books borrowed by genre","categories":["Fiction","Mystery","Science","History","Poetry"],"series":[{"name":"Books","values":[420,260,180,140,50]}],"yLabel":"Books"},"opts":["17.1%","35.7%","42.9%","57.1%","233.3%"],"correct":2,"move":"\"A is what percent of B\" means A / B, with B after the word \"of.\"","why":"Science / Fiction = 180 / 420 = 0.4286, or 42.9%. The reverse, 420 / 180, is 233.3%. Science out of all books (1,050) is 17.1%. And 57.1% is how much smaller science is than fiction.","diag":{"0":"That compares science with all 1,050 books, not with fiction.","4":"That is fiction as a percent of science. The base is the number after \"of\": fiction."}},{"id":"di-025","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"For the full year, West's total units sold were what percent of North's total, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Units sold by store and quarter","categories":["Q1","Q2","Q3","Q4"],"series":[{"name":"North","values":[30,45,40,55]},{"name":"South","values":[35,30,50,45]},{"name":"West","values":[20,35,30,40]}],"yLabel":"Units"},"opts":["26.5%","36.0%","73.5%","78.1%","136.0%"],"correct":2,"move":"Total each series across the quarters before comparing the stores.","why":"North: 30 + 45 + 40 + 55 = 170. West: 20 + 35 + 30 + 40 = 125. West as a percent of North: 125 / 170 = 0.7353, or 73.5%. Comparing West to South (160) instead gives 78.1%.","diag":{"3":"That compares West with South. The question compares West with North.","4":"That is North as a percent of West. The base here is North."}},{"id":"di-026","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":2,"stem":"What was the business's total profit for the five months shown, in thousands of dollars?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Monthly profit of a small business (thousands of dollars)","categories":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May"],"series":[{"name":"Profit","values":[12,-4,8,-6,15]}],"yLabel":"Thousands of dollars"},"opts":["15","25","35","45","49"],"correct":1,"move":"Bars below the axis are negative: subtract them.","why":"Add with signs: 12 + (-4) + 8 + (-6) + 15 = 25 thousand dollars. Adding the losses as if they were gains gives 12 + 4 + 8 + 6 + 15 = 45.","diag":{"2":"Only the positive months were counted. Include the two losses.","3":"February and April fall below zero; they are losses and must be subtracted."}},{"id":"di-027","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"On how many of the days shown was the step count above the mean for the week?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Steps walked per day (thousands)","categories":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"name":"Steps","values":[6,8,5,10,7,12,9]}],"yLabel":"Thousands of steps"},"opts":["2","3","4","5","6"],"correct":1,"move":"Find the mean first, then count bars strictly above it.","why":"Mean: (6 + 8 + 5 + 10 + 7 + 12 + 9) / 7 = 57 / 7, about 8.14 thousand. Days above 8.14: Thursday (10), Saturday (12), and Sunday (9). Tuesday at 8 is just under. Three days.","diag":{"2":"Tuesday (8 thousand) is just below the mean of about 8.14."}},{"id":"di-028","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"For which age group is the weekend average the greatest percent above the weekday average?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Average nightly sleep by age group","categories":["18-24","25-34","35-44","45-54","55-64"],"series":[{"name":"Weekday","values":[6.5,7,6.5,6,6.5]},{"name":"Weekend","values":[9,8.5,7.5,7,7.5]}],"yLabel":"Hours"},"opts":["18-24","25-34","35-44","45-54","55-64"],"correct":0,"move":"Same idea as percent change: (weekend - weekday) / weekday for each group.","why":"18-24: 2.5 / 6.5, about 38.5%. 25-34: 1.5 / 7, about 21.4%. 35-44: 1 / 6.5, about 15.4%. 45-54: 1 / 6, about 16.7%. 55-64: 1 / 6.5, about 15.4%. The 18-24 group has the biggest relative jump."},{"id":"di-029","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"If the same proportions hold for a group of 330 students, how many of them would choose the library?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Favorite study location of surveyed students","categories":["Library","Home","Cafe","Dorm","Park"],"series":[{"name":"Students","values":[48,72,30,54,16]}],"yLabel":"Students","orientation":"horizontal"},"opts":["72","80","96","108","120"],"correct":0,"move":"Scale a share: (part / survey total) × new total.","why":"Survey total: 48 + 72 + 30 + 54 + 16 = 220. Library share: 48 / 220. For 330 students: 48 / 220 × 330 = 48 × 1.5 = 72. Note 72 is also the Home bar, so read the question, not the chart, to know which group you want.","diag":{"3":"That scales the Home count (72 × 1.5), not the Library count."}},{"id":"di-030","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"graphical","diff":3,"stem":"If the population continues to grow by the same amount every 5 years, in what year will it first reach 45 thousand?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Population of a town (thousands)","categories":["2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"series":[{"name":"Population","values":[24,27,30,33,36]}],"yLabel":"Thousands"},"opts":["2030","2035","2040","2045","2050"],"correct":1,"move":"Constant step: find the step per interval, then count steps to the target.","why":"The population rises 3 thousand every 5 years. From 36 thousand in 2020 to 45 thousand is 9 thousand more, which is 3 steps of 5 years. 2020 + 15 = 2035. Check: 2025 is 39, 2030 is 42, 2035 is 45.","diag":{"0":"2030 gives 42 thousand. One more 5-year step is needed to reach 45."}},{"id":"di-031","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"Which program had the highest acceptance rate (accepted divided by applied)?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Applications and acceptances by program","categories":["Nursing","Biology","Chemistry","Physics","Math"],"series":[{"name":"Applied","values":[400,300,200,100,150]},{"name":"Accepted","values":[120,105,80,45,60]}],"yLabel":"Students"},"opts":["Nursing","Biology","Chemistry","Physics","Math"],"correct":3,"move":"Rate = part / whole for each program; the biggest bars do not decide it.","why":"Nursing 120 / 400 = 30%. Biology 105 / 300 = 35%. Chemistry 80 / 200 = 40%. Physics 45 / 100 = 45%. Math 60 / 150 = 40%. Physics has the highest rate despite the fewest acceptances.","diag":{"0":"Nursing accepted the most students, but out of 400 applicants that is only 30%."}},{"id":"di-032","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":4,"stem":"The value falls by the same percent each year. If that continues, what will the bicycle be worth at 7 years old, to the nearest dollar?","figure":{"type":"bar","title":"Resale value of a bicycle by age","categories":["1 yr","2 yr","3 yr","4 yr","5 yr"],"series":[{"name":"Dollars","values":[400,340,289,245.65,208.8]}],"yLabel":"Dollars"},"opts":["151 dollars","160 dollars","177 dollars","209 dollars","245 dollars"],"correct":0,"move":"Constant percent drop: find the yearly multiplier, then raise it to the number of years.","why":"From 400 to 340 is a drop of 60, or 15%, so each year multiplies the value by 0.85 (340 to 289 is also 15%). From age 1 to age 7 is 6 years: 400 × 0.85^6 = 400 × 0.37715 = 150.86, about 151 dollars. Subtracting 60 every year (a straight line) gives 40, and stopping at 6 years old gives 177.","diag":{"2":"That is the value at 6 years old (five steps from age 1). Age 7 needs six steps.","3":"That is the value shown for age 5. Two more years of 15% drops remain."}},{"id":"di-033","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"rates","diff":2,"stem":"What was the average rate of cooling, in degrees per minute, from minute 5 to minute 20, to the nearest hundredth?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Temperature of a cooling cup of tea","x":["0","5","10","15","20","25"],"series":[{"name":"Degrees C","values":[90,74,62,53,46,41]}],"yLabel":"Degrees C","xLabel":"Minutes"},"opts":["0.93","1.47","1.87","1.96","5.6"],"correct":2,"move":"Average rate = change in value / change in time, using only the two endpoints asked for.","why":"At minute 5 the tea is 74 degrees; at minute 20 it is 46. Change: 28 degrees over 15 minutes. 28 / 15 = 1.867, about 1.87 degrees per minute. Using the whole graph (49 over 25) gives 1.96, which answers a different interval.","diag":{"3":"That uses minutes 0 to 25. The question asks about minutes 5 to 20.","4":"Divide by the 15 minutes elapsed, not by the 5-minute spacing between points."}},{"id":"di-034","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"graphical","diff":2,"stem":"In which month did App B first have more subscribers than App A?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Monthly subscribers to two apps (thousands)","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"name":"App A","values":[20,25,30,35,40,45]},{"name":"App B","values":[5,10,20,30,40,50]}],"yLabel":"Thousands","valueLabels":false},"opts":["Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"correct":4,"move":"Where two lines meet is a tie, not a lead; look for the first point strictly above.","why":"Read both lines month by month. Through April, App A is ahead. In May both are at 40 thousand, a tie. In June App B reaches 50 thousand against 45 for App A, its first month in front.","diag":{"3":"In May the lines meet at 40 thousand. Equal is not more."}},{"id":"di-035","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"rates","diff":4,"stem":"Define each app's growth factor as its June total divided by its January total. App B's growth factor is how many times App A's growth factor, to the nearest hundredth?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Monthly subscribers to two apps (thousands)","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"name":"App A","values":[20,25,30,35,40,45]},{"name":"App B","values":[5,10,20,30,40,50]}],"yLabel":"Thousands","valueLabels":true},"opts":["2.25","4.44","6.25","8","10"],"correct":1,"move":"Growth factor = end / start for each line; then compare the two factors.","why":"App B grew from 5 to 50, a factor of 10. App A grew from 20 to 45, a factor of 2.25. Compare: 10 / 2.25 = 4.444, so App B's growth factor is about 4.44 times App A's. The numbers 10 and 2.25 alone are each app's own factor.","diag":{"4":"That is App B's own growth factor. The question compares it with App A's factor of 2.25."}},{"id":"di-036","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"rates","diff":4,"stem":"The average rate of product formation during the first 20 minutes was how many times the average rate from minute 20 to minute 50, to the nearest hundredth?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Product concentration during a reaction","x":["0","10","20","30","40","50"],"series":[{"name":"mM","values":[0,18,32,42,48,50]}],"yLabel":"Concentration (mM)","xLabel":"Minutes"},"opts":["0.38","1.6","1.78","2.67","4"],"correct":3,"move":"Compare rates, not changes: divide each change by its own time window.","why":"First 20 minutes: 32 mM / 20 min = 1.6 mM per minute. Minutes 20 to 50: (50 - 32) / 30 = 18 / 30 = 0.6 mM per minute. Ratio: 1.6 / 0.6 = 2.667, about 2.67. Comparing the raw changes (32 vs 18) gives 1.78, which ignores the different window lengths.","diag":{"2":"The windows are 20 and 30 minutes long; divide each change by its own time first."}},{"id":"di-037","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"exponents","diff":3,"stem":"If the culture keeps growing in the same pattern, after how many whole hours will it first exceed 1,000 thousand bacteria?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Bacteria in a culture (thousands)","x":["0","1","2","3","4","5"],"series":[{"name":"Thousands","values":[2,4,8,16,32,64]}],"yLabel":"Thousands","xLabel":"Hours"},"opts":["9","10","12","15","34"],"correct":0,"move":"Doubling: keep doubling from the last point and count the hours until you pass the target.","why":"The count doubles every hour. Continue from 64 at hour 5: 128 at hour 6, 256 at 7, 512 at 8, 1,024 at 9. Hour 9 is the first to exceed 1,000. A straight-line guess using the last jump (32 per hour) would take about 29 more hours.","diag":{"1":"Count the doublings from hour 5: 128, 256, 512, 1,024. Hour 9 already passes 1,000."}},{"id":"di-038","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":3,"stem":"In how many of the months shown was City Z's average temperature lower than that of both other cities?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Average monthly temperature in three cities","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"name":"City X","values":[30,35,45,55,65,75]},{"name":"City Y","values":[50,50,55,60,65,70]},{"name":"City Z","values":[10,15,30,45,60,70]}],"yLabel":"Degrees F","yStep":10,"valueLabels":false},"opts":["2","3","4","5","6"],"correct":3,"move":"\"Lower than both\" means beat each other line separately in that month; ties fail.","why":"Check City Z against X and Y each month. Jan 10 (vs 30, 50): yes. Feb 15 (35, 50): yes. Mar 30 (45, 55): yes. Apr 45 (55, 60): yes. May 60 (65, 65): yes. Jun 70 (75, 70): no, it ties City Y. Five months.","diag":{"4":"In June City Z (70) equals City Y (70), so it is not lower than both."}},{"id":"di-039","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"Between which two consecutive years did the ticket price increase by the greatest percent?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Concert ticket price by year","x":["2018","2019","2020","2021","2022","2023"],"series":[{"name":"Dollars","values":[20,22,22,25,29,30]}],"yLabel":"Dollars"},"opts":["2018-2019","2019-2020","2020-2021","2021-2022","2022-2023"],"correct":3,"move":"Steepest percent climb = biggest (change / starting value), checked pair by pair.","why":"2018-2019: 2 / 20 = 10%. 2019-2020: 0%. 2020-2021: 3 / 22, about 13.6%. 2021-2022: 4 / 25 = 16%. 2022-2023: 1 / 29, about 3.4%. The largest is 2021 to 2022.","diag":{"2":"A 3 dollar rise on 22 is about 13.6%; the next year rose 4 dollars on 25, which is 16%."}},{"id":"di-040","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"graphical","diff":2,"stem":"If the level keeps falling at the same rate, in which week will it first fall below 6 meters?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Water level in a reservoir","x":["1","2","3","4","5","6","7","8"],"series":[{"name":"Meters","values":[12,11.5,11,10.5,10,9.5,9,8.5]}],"yLabel":"Meters","xLabel":"Week"},"opts":["11","12","13","14","15"],"correct":3,"move":"\"Below\" a level is the step after you reach it exactly.","why":"The level drops 0.5 meters per week. From 8.5 meters in week 8, it takes (8.5 - 6) / 0.5 = 5 more weeks to reach exactly 6 meters, in week 13. It first falls below 6 one week later, in week 14 (5.5 meters).","diag":{"2":"In week 13 the level is exactly 6 meters. Below 6 happens in week 14."}},{"id":"di-041","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"In week 6, studying time was what percent of the total time spent on studying and gaming combined, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Weekly hours: studying vs gaming","x":["Wk 1","Wk 2","Wk 3","Wk 4","Wk 5","Wk 6"],"series":[{"name":"Studying","values":[10,12,14,16,18,20]},{"name":"Gaming","values":[22,19,16,13,10,7]}],"yLabel":"Hours","valueLabels":true},"opts":["25.9%","35.0%","65.0%","74.1%","285.7%"],"correct":3,"move":"Percent of a combined total: part / (part + other part).","why":"Week 6: studying 20 hours, gaming 7 hours, total 27. Studying share: 20 / 27 = 0.7407, or 74.1%. Gaming is the other 25.9%. Comparing studying with gaming alone gives 20 / 7 = 285.7%, which answers a different question.","diag":{"4":"That is studying as a percent of gaming. The base is the combined total, 27 hours."}},{"id":"di-042","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"data","diff":3,"stem":"The graph shows the running total of miles. How many miles did the runner run during week 4 alone?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Total miles run since the start of a training plan","x":["Wk 1","Wk 2","Wk 3","Wk 4","Wk 5","Wk 6"],"series":[{"name":"Miles","values":[15,32,51,72,95,120]}],"yLabel":"Total miles"},"opts":["18","21","24","51","72"],"correct":1,"move":"A running total: one period is the difference between consecutive points.","why":"The graph is cumulative, so each point is everything run so far. Week 4 alone is the total after week 4 minus the total after week 3: 72 - 51 = 21 miles. Reading 72 straight off the graph gives the total through week 4, not week 4 itself.","diag":{"4":"That is the total through week 4. Subtract the 51 miles already run by the end of week 3."}},{"id":"di-043","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":2,"stem":"What is the median of the eight quarterly unemployment rates shown?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Quarterly unemployment rate","x":["Q1","Q2","Q3","Q4","Q5","Q6","Q7","Q8"],"series":[{"name":"Rate","values":[5.2,5,4.8,4.9,4.6,4.4,4.5,4]}],"yLabel":"Percent","unit":"%","yMin":3.5,"yMax":5.5,"yStep":0.5},"opts":["4.5%","4.6%","4.65%","4.675%","4.7%"],"correct":4,"move":"Median: sort first; with eight values, average the 4th and 5th.","why":"Sorted: 4.0, 4.4, 4.5, 4.6, 4.8, 4.9, 5.0, 5.2. The median is the mean of the 4th and 5th values: (4.6 + 4.8) / 2 = 4.7%. The mean of all eight is 37.4 / 8 = 4.675%, a different measure.","diag":{"3":"That is the mean. The median averages only the two middle sorted values."}},{"id":"di-044","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"The range of the readings is what percent of the mean reading, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Daily air quality index readings","x":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"name":"AQI","values":[42,55,61,48,70,66,52]}],"yLabel":"AQI"},"opts":["40.0%","42.4%","45.4%","46.7%","49.7%"],"correct":4,"move":"Do the two statistics separately, then divide: range / mean.","why":"Range: 70 - 42 = 28. Mean: (42 + 55 + 61 + 48 + 70 + 66 + 52) / 7 = 394 / 7, about 56.29. Range as a percent of the mean: 28 / 56.29 = 0.4975, or 49.7%. Using the median (55) instead of the mean gives 50.9%.","diag":{"0":"That divides by the highest reading, 70. The question asks for the mean."}},{"id":"di-045","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"At week 5, by what percent did the fertilized plant's height exceed the unfertilized plant's height, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Plant height with and without fertilizer","x":["0","1","2","3","4","5"],"series":[{"name":"Fertilized","values":[2,4,7,11,15,20]},{"name":"Unfertilized","values":[2,3,5,7,9,11]}],"yLabel":"Height (cm)","xLabel":"Week","valueLabels":true},"opts":["45.0%","55.0%","81.8%","100.0%","181.8%"],"correct":2,"move":"\"Exceeds by what percent\" = difference / the smaller (compared) value.","why":"Week 5: fertilized 20 cm, unfertilized 11 cm. Difference 9 cm. Percent above the unfertilized plant: 9 / 11 = 0.818, or 81.8%. Dividing by 20 gives 45.0%; 20 / 11 = 181.8% is the ratio, not the excess.","diag":{"0":"The comparison is to the unfertilized plant, so divide by 11.","4":"Subtract 100%: 181.8% of the height means 81.8% more."}},{"id":"di-046","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"rates","diff":2,"stem":"What was the average change in daily visitors per day from Day 1 to Day 7, in thousands, to the nearest hundredth?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Website visitors per day (thousands)","x":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4","Day 5","Day 6","Day 7"],"series":[{"name":"Visitors","values":[12,15,13,18,22,20,25]}],"yLabel":"Thousands"},"opts":["1.86","2.17","3.57","13","17.86"],"correct":1,"move":"Average change per step = (last - first) / number of steps, and 7 points have 6 steps.","why":"From Day 1 (12 thousand) to Day 7 (25 thousand) is a change of 13 thousand over 6 day-to-day steps. 13 / 6 = 2.167, about 2.17 thousand per day. Dividing by 7 (the number of points) gives 1.86.","diag":{"0":"Seven days have six gaps between them. Divide 13 by 6."}},{"id":"di-047","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"interest","diff":4,"stem":"Sales grew by the same percent each year. If that continues, what will sales be in 2026, to the nearest thousand units?","figure":{"type":"line","title":"Annual sales (thousands of units)","x":["2019","2020","2021","2022","2023","2024"],"series":[{"name":"Sales","values":[50,55,60.5,66.55,73.205,80.5255]}],"yLabel":"Thousands of units"},"opts":["97","101","107","112","118"],"correct":0,"move":"Constant percent growth multiplies by the same factor every year; extend with the factor, not the last jump.","why":"From 50 to 55 is 10% growth, and each later year is 1.1 times the one before. 2026 is two years after 2024: 80.53 × 1.1 × 1.1 = 97.44, about 97 thousand. Adding the last increase (about 7.3 thousand) twice gives about 95, which undercounts because the jumps keep growing.","diag":{"2":"That is three years past 2024 (2027). 2026 is two years past 2024."}},{"id":"di-048","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":1,"stem":"According to the chart, what percent of the student's monthly spending goes to food and transport combined?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"A student's monthly spending (dollars)","show":"value","slices":[{"label":"Rent","value":600},{"label":"Food","value":300},{"label":"Transport","value":120},{"label":"Books","value":80},{"label":"Other","value":100}]},"opts":["14%","20%","25%","30%","35%"],"correct":4,"move":"Add the slices you need, then divide by the whole pie.","why":"Food and transport: 300 + 120 = 420 dollars. Total: 600 + 300 + 120 + 80 + 100 = 1,200 dollars. 420 / 1,200 = 0.35, or 35%. Comparing with rent alone (420 / 600) gives 70%.","diag":{"2":"That is food alone (300 / 1,200). Add transport too."}},{"id":"di-049","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"A 600-gram bag of this trail mix contains how many more grams of peanuts than almonds?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Trail mix by weight","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Peanuts","value":40},{"label":"Raisins","value":25},{"label":"Almonds","value":20},{"label":"Chocolate","value":15}]},"opts":["120","150","180","200","240"],"correct":0,"move":"Percent points to grams: (difference in percent) × total.","why":"Peanuts are 40% and almonds 20%, a difference of 20 percentage points. 20% of 600 grams is 120 grams. Check: peanuts 240 g, almonds 120 g, difference 120 g.","diag":{"4":"That is the peanut weight alone. Subtract the 120 g of almonds."}},{"id":"di-050","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"How many more donors in the sample had type O blood than had type B or type AB?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Blood types in a sample of 250 donors","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"O","value":44.8},{"label":"A","value":38},{"label":"B","value":11.2},{"label":"AB","value":6}]},"opts":["69","82","98","112","155"],"correct":0,"move":"Turn each percent into a count by multiplying by the sample size, then compare counts.","why":"Type O: 44.8% of 250 = 112 donors. Types B and AB: 11.2% + 6% = 17.2% of 250 = 43 donors. Difference: 112 - 43 = 69. Equivalently, 27.6 percentage points of 250 is 69.","diag":{"3":"That is the type O count alone. Subtract the 43 donors with B or AB."}},{"id":"di-051","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":4,"stem":"The fund totals 2.4 million dollars. Next year the total stays the same, equipment spending doubles, and the extra equipment money is taken equally from programs and staff. How much will go to programs, in thousands of dollars?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"How a city park fund is divided","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Maintenance","value":45},{"label":"Programs","value":25},{"label":"Staff","value":20},{"label":"Equipment","value":10}]},"opts":["360","480","540","600","720"],"correct":1,"move":"Work in dollars, move the money, then read the new slice.","why":"Equipment is 10% of 2,400 thousand = 240 thousand; doubling adds 240 thousand. Half of that, 120 thousand, comes from programs. Programs start at 25% of 2,400 = 600 thousand and end at 600 - 120 = 480 thousand dollars.","diag":{"0":"Only half of the extra 240 thousand comes from programs; the other half comes from staff.","3":"That is this year's amount before the shift."}},{"id":"di-052","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"Next year Dune's share doubles, and every point Dune gains comes from Apex. What will the ratio of Apex's share to Dune's share be?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Market share of five carriers","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Apex","value":32},{"label":"Beacon","value":27},{"label":"Crest","value":21},{"label":"Dune","value":12},{"label":"Echo","value":8}]},"opts":["2/3","5/6","1","6/5","8/3"],"correct":1,"move":"Shift the points first, then form the ratio from the new shares.","why":"Dune goes from 12% to 24%, gaining 12 points. Apex loses those 12 points: 32% - 12% = 20%. The ratio of Apex to Dune is 20 / 24 = 5/6. Using the old shares gives 32 / 12 = 8/3.","diag":{"3":"That is Dune to Apex. The question asks Apex to Dune.","4":"That uses this year's shares. Move the 12 points from Apex to Dune first."}},{"id":"di-053","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"What fraction of the non-break time in the session was spent on problems?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Time use in a 60-minute study session","show":"value","unit":"min","slices":[{"label":"Reading","value":24},{"label":"Problems","value":18},{"label":"Review","value":12},{"label":"Break","value":6}]},"opts":["1/6","1/4","5/18","3/10","1/3"],"correct":4,"move":"Shrink the whole first when the question removes a slice: \"non-break\" means total minus break.","why":"Non-break time: 60 - 6 = 54 minutes. Problems: 18 minutes. 18 / 54 = 1/3. Using the full hour gives 18 / 60 = 3/10.","diag":{"3":"That divides by all 60 minutes. Remove the 6-minute break first."}},{"id":"di-054","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"The school used 80,000 kWh in total. Next year total use stays the same, and solar output rises by 4,000 kWh, replacing grid power. What percent of the total will solar supply?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Sources of a school's electricity","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Grid","value":55},{"label":"Solar","value":25},{"label":"Wind","value":15},{"label":"Generator","value":5}]},"opts":["30%","31.6%","35%","45%","55%"],"correct":0,"move":"Percent to amount, adjust the amount, then back to percent of the (unchanged) total.","why":"Solar now: 25% of 80,000 = 20,000 kWh. Next year: 20,000 + 4,000 = 24,000 kWh. The total stays 80,000, so solar supplies 24,000 / 80,000 = 30%. Dividing by 76,000 (as if the total shrank) gives 31.6%.","diag":{"1":"The total stays at 80,000 because solar replaces grid power one for one."}},{"id":"di-055","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"What is the ratio of cat households to fish and bird households combined?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Pets in 160 surveyed households","show":"value","slices":[{"label":"Dog","value":64},{"label":"Cat","value":48},{"label":"Fish","value":24},{"label":"Bird","value":16},{"label":"Other","value":8}]},"opts":["3/5","2/3","5/6","1","6/5"],"correct":4,"move":"Combine the second group before forming the ratio.","why":"Fish and bird together: 24 + 16 = 40. Cat: 48. Ratio 48 / 40 = 6/5. Flipping it gives 5/6, and comparing cat with fish alone gives 2.","diag":{"2":"That is fish and bird to cat, the reverse order."}},{"id":"di-056","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":1,"stem":"How many students in the class earned a grade of C or better?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Final grades in a class of 40 students","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"A","value":15},{"label":"B","value":35},{"label":"C","value":30},{"label":"D","value":15},{"label":"F","value":5}]},"opts":["12","20","26","32","34"],"correct":3,"move":"\"C or better\" means C, B, and A together.","why":"A + B + C = 15% + 35% + 30% = 80%. 80% of 40 students is 32. Leaving out C gives 50%, or 20 students.","diag":{"1":"Include the C grades: \"or better\" keeps C itself."}},{"id":"di-057","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"If the nonprofit spent 45,000 dollars on admin, how much did it spend on programs?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"A nonprofit's yearly spending","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Programs","value":72},{"label":"Fundraising","value":18},{"label":"Admin","value":10}]},"opts":["81,000 dollars","108,000 dollars","162,000 dollars","250,000 dollars","324,000 dollars"],"correct":4,"move":"Use the known slice to find the whole, or scale directly by the ratio of percents.","why":"Admin is 10%, so the total is 45,000 / 0.10 = 450,000 dollars. Programs are 72% of that: 0.72 × 450,000 = 324,000 dollars. Shortcut: programs / admin = 72 / 10 = 7.2, and 7.2 × 45,000 = 324,000.","diag":{"0":"That is fundraising, 18% of the 450,000 dollar total."}},{"id":"di-058","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":4,"stem":"Next month paper drops by one third and metal doubles, while plastic and glass stay the same. What percent of the new total will be metal, to the nearest tenth of a percent?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Recycled material by weight","show":"value","unit":"tons","slices":[{"label":"Paper","value":18},{"label":"Plastic","value":9},{"label":"Glass","value":6},{"label":"Metal","value":3}]},"opts":["8.3%","12.5%","15.4%","16.7%","18.2%"],"correct":4,"move":"Rebuild the whole after a change; the old total is no longer the base.","why":"New amounts: paper 18 - 6 = 12, plastic 9, glass 6, metal 3 × 2 = 6. New total: 12 + 9 + 6 + 6 = 33 tons. Metal: 6 / 33 = 0.1818, or 18.2%. Dividing by the old total, 36, gives 16.7%.","diag":{"3":"The total changed to 33 tons. Divide by the new total."}},{"id":"di-059","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"How many more students walk to campus than bike or take the train?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"How 600 students get to campus","show":"percent","slices":[{"label":"Walk","value":30},{"label":"Bus","value":25},{"label":"Car","value":20},{"label":"Bike","value":15},{"label":"Train","value":10}]},"opts":["10","30","60","90","150"],"correct":1,"move":"Subtract percentages first, then convert once: (30% - 25%) of 600.","why":"Walk: 30%. Bike or train: 15% + 10% = 25%. Difference: 5 percentage points. 5% of 600 = 30 students.","diag":{"3":"That compares walking with biking only (15 points). Train riders count too."}},{"id":"di-060","format":"di","skill":"data-figs","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"A student finishes every section in 80% of its allotted time. In total, how many minutes of the exam go unused?","figure":{"type":"pie","title":"Sections of a 120-minute practice exam","show":"value","unit":"min","slices":[{"label":"Section 1","value":45},{"label":"Section 2","value":30},{"label":"Section 3","value":25},{"label":"Section 4","value":20}]},"opts":["24","30","36","48","96"],"correct":0,"move":"If every part shrinks by the same percent, apply it to the whole at once.","why":"Each section uses 80% of its time, so 20% of every section goes unused. Since that is the same for all sections, 20% of the whole 120 minutes is unused: 24 minutes. 96 is the time used, not the time left over.","diag":{"4":"That is the time the student used. The question asks for the unused time."}},{"id":"aw-001","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":3,"stem":"Pump P can fill an empty tank in 6 hours, and pump Q can fill the same tank in 9 hours. Pump P runs alone for 2 hours, and then pump Q is turned on as well. How many hours after pump P started will the tank be full?","opts":["2.4 hours","3.6 hours","4.4 hours","4.8 hours","5.6 hours"],"correct":2,"move":"Work in rates (tanks per hour); rates add, times do not.","why":"Pump P fills 1/6 of the tank per hour, so in 2 hours it fills 2/6 = 1/3. Two thirds remain. Together the pumps fill 1/6 + 1/9 = 3/18 + 2/18 = 5/18 of the tank per hour. Time for the rest: (2/3) / (5/18) = (2/3) × (18/5) = 12/5 = 2.4 hours. Add the first 2 hours: 2 + 2.4 = 4.4 hours after pump P started.","diag":{"0":"That is only the time both pumps run together. Add the 2 hours pump P ran alone.","1":"That is how long both pumps would need for a full empty tank (18/5). Part of the tank was already filled."}},{"id":"aw-002","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":2,"stem":"A lab has 30 liters of a 10% saline solution. How many liters of a 40% saline solution must be added to produce a mixture that is 20% saline?","opts":["10 liters","12 liters","15 liters","18 liters","20 liters"],"correct":2,"move":"Track the pure stuff: amount of salt before = amount of salt after.","why":"Let x be the liters of 40% solution. Salt in: 0.10(30) + 0.40x = 3 + 0.4x. Salt out: 0.20(30 + x) = 6 + 0.2x. Set them equal: 3 + 0.4x = 6 + 0.2x, so 0.2x = 3 and x = 15 liters. Check: 3 + 6 = 9 liters of salt in 45 liters is 20%.","diag":{"0":"Splitting the difference evenly ignores how far each solution sits from 20%. The 40% solution is twice as far, so you need half as much as the 30 liters."}},{"id":"aw-003","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"A store sets the list price of a jacket 60% above its cost. During a sale the jacket sells for 25% off the list price, and the store makes a profit of 18 dollars on the sale. What was the store's cost for the jacket?","opts":["51.43 dollars","72 dollars","75 dollars","90 dollars","100 dollars"],"correct":3,"move":"Chain percent changes as multipliers: up 60% is × 1.6, down 25% is × 0.75.","why":"Let the cost be c. List price = 1.6c. Sale price = 0.75 × 1.6c = 1.2c. Profit = 1.2c - c = 0.2c. So 0.2c = 18 and c = 90 dollars. The shortcut trap: +60% then -25% is not +35%; multiplied, it is +20%.","diag":{"0":"You added the percents (+60% - 25% = +35%) and solved 0.35c = 18. Percent changes multiply: 1.6 × 0.75 = 1.2, a 20% gain.","1":"Check by plugging in: 72 × 1.2 = 86.4, a profit of 14.4, not 18."}},{"id":"aw-004","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":3,"stem":"A cyclist rides 30 miles from home to a lake at an average speed of 15 miles per hour and returns home along the same route at an average speed of 10 miles per hour. What is the cyclist's average speed for the entire round trip, in miles per hour?","opts":["12","12.5","13","14","15"],"correct":0,"move":"Average speed is total distance over total time, never the average of the two speeds.","why":"Time out: 30 / 15 = 2 hours. Time back: 30 / 10 = 3 hours. Total: 60 miles in 5 hours, so 60 / 5 = 12 miles per hour. Averaging the speeds gives 12.5, which is too high because the cyclist spends more time at the slower speed.","diag":{"1":"That averages 15 and 10. The slow leg takes longer, so it counts for more of the trip."}},{"id":"aw-005","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":3,"stem":"Train A leaves a station at 1:00 p.m. traveling at 60 miles per hour. Train B leaves the same station at 2:30 p.m. on a parallel track in the same direction, traveling at 80 miles per hour. How many hours after Train B departs will it catch up to Train A?","opts":["1.5","2.25","3","3.75","4.5"],"correct":4,"move":"Catch-up: head start distance / difference in speeds.","why":"By 2:30 Train A has run 1.5 hours at 60 mph, a 90-mile head start. Train B closes the gap at 80 - 60 = 20 miles per hour. Time to close 90 miles: 90 / 20 = 4.5 hours after Train B leaves, at 7:00 p.m.","diag":{"0":"That is the head start in time. The gap closes at only 20 miles per hour."}},{"id":"aw-006","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":2,"stem":"Two runners start at opposite ends of a 12-mile trail at the same time and run toward each other. One runs at 7 miles per hour and the other at 5 miles per hour. How many miles from the faster runner's starting point do they meet?","opts":["5","6","6.5","7","8.4"],"correct":3,"move":"Moving toward each other: speeds add. Find the meeting time, then one runner's distance.","why":"They close the gap at 7 + 5 = 12 miles per hour, so they meet after 12 / 12 = 1 hour. In 1 hour the faster runner covers 7 miles. Check: the slower runner covers 5, and 7 + 5 = 12.","diag":{"0":"That is the slower runner's distance.","1":"Halfway assumes equal speeds. The faster runner covers more of the trail."}},{"id":"aw-007","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":4,"stem":"A delivery van drives 120 miles at 40 miles per hour, stops for half an hour, and then drives another 90 miles at 60 miles per hour. What is the van's average speed for the whole trip, including the stop, in miles per hour?","opts":["42","45","46.67","50","52.5"],"correct":0,"move":"Every minute on the clock counts toward total time, including stops.","why":"Leg 1: 120 / 40 = 3 hours. Stop: 0.5 hours. Leg 2: 90 / 60 = 1.5 hours. Total time: 5 hours for 210 miles, so 210 / 5 = 42 miles per hour. Leaving out the stop gives 210 / 4.5 = 46.67; averaging 40 and 60 gives 50.","diag":{"2":"The question says including the stop, so add the half hour to the total time.","3":"That averages the two speeds; use total distance over total time."}},{"id":"aw-008","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":2,"stem":"A boat travels 36 miles downstream in 2 hours. The return trip upstream along the same route takes 3 hours. Assuming the boat's speed in still water and the speed of the current are constant, what is the speed of the current, in miles per hour?","opts":["2","3","5","6","15"],"correct":1,"move":"Downstream = boat + current, upstream = boat - current; half the difference is the current.","why":"Downstream speed: 36 / 2 = 18 mph. Upstream speed: 36 / 3 = 12 mph. Boat + current = 18 and boat - current = 12. Subtract: 2 × current = 6, so the current is 3 mph (and the boat is 15 mph).","diag":{"3":"That is the difference between the two speeds, which equals twice the current.","4":"That is the boat's speed in still water."}},{"id":"aw-009","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"rates","diff":3,"stem":"Two cars leave the same point at the same time and drive in opposite directions. One car is 15 miles per hour faster than the other. After 2.5 hours they are 275 miles apart. What is the speed of the slower car, in miles per hour?","opts":["40","47.5","55","62.5","110"],"correct":1,"move":"Opposite directions: the distance apart grows at the sum of the speeds.","why":"Let v be the slower speed; the faster is v + 15. Together they separate at 2v + 15 mph. In 2.5 hours: 2.5(2v + 15) = 275, so 2v + 15 = 110, 2v = 95, and v = 47.5. The faster car goes 62.5.","diag":{"3":"That is the faster car. The slower one is 15 mph less.","4":"That is the combined speed, 275 / 2.5."}},{"id":"aw-010","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":3,"stem":"An inlet pipe can fill an empty tank in 4 hours, and a drain can empty a full tank in 6 hours. If the tank starts empty and both the inlet and the drain are open, how many hours will it take to fill the tank?","opts":["2.4","5","10","12","24"],"correct":3,"move":"A drain is a negative rate: subtract it.","why":"Inlet: +1/4 tank per hour. Drain: -1/6 tank per hour. Net: 1/4 - 1/6 = 3/12 - 2/12 = 1/12 tank per hour. Filling one tank takes 12 hours. Adding the rates as if both helped gives 2.4 hours.","diag":{"0":"The drain works against the inlet; subtract its rate instead of adding it."}},{"id":"aw-011","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":3,"stem":"Six painters, all working at the same rate, can paint a building in 10 days. After the six painters have worked for 4 days, 3 more painters who work at the same rate join them. In total, how many days does the job take from the start?","opts":["6","7","8","8.5","9"],"correct":2,"move":"Count work in worker-days: total job = workers × days.","why":"The job is 6 × 10 = 60 painter-days. In 4 days, 6 painters do 24 painter-days, leaving 36. Nine painters finish 36 painter-days in 36 / 9 = 4 days. Total: 4 + 4 = 8 days."},{"id":"aw-012","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":4,"stem":"Machine A can complete an order in 12 hours and machine B can complete the same order in 8 hours. Both machines start together, but after 3 hours machine A breaks down and machine B finishes the order alone. How many hours does the whole order take?","opts":["4.8","6","7.5","8","9"],"correct":1,"move":"Split the job into phases: work done = rate × time in each phase, and the phases add to 1.","why":"Together for 3 hours: 3(1/12 + 1/8) = 3(2/24 + 3/24) = 15/24 = 5/8 of the order. Remaining: 3/8. Machine B alone does 1/8 per hour, so it needs 3 more hours. Total: 3 + 3 = 6 hours. 4.8 hours is the time if both machines worked the whole way.","diag":{"0":"That is the together time with no breakdown. Machine A stopped after 3 hours."}},{"id":"aw-013","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":2,"stem":"Pat can sort a shipment alone in 5 hours. Working together, Pat and Sam can sort the same shipment in 3 hours. How many hours would Sam take to sort the shipment alone?","opts":["2","4","6.5","7.5","8"],"correct":3,"move":"Together rate minus one worker's rate leaves the other worker's rate.","why":"Together: 1/3 shipment per hour. Pat: 1/5. Sam: 1/3 - 1/5 = 5/15 - 3/15 = 2/15 shipment per hour. Time alone: 15/2 = 7.5 hours. Subtracting the times (5 - 3 = 2) is a common slip.","diag":{"0":"Times do not subtract; rates do. 1/3 - 1/5 = 2/15."}},{"id":"aw-014","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":2,"stem":"One printer can print 1,800 pages in 45 minutes, and a second printer can print 1,800 pages in 30 minutes. Working together at these rates, how many minutes will the two printers take to print 3,000 pages?","opts":["25","30","37.5","45","75"],"correct":1,"move":"Turn each machine into pages per minute, add, then divide the job by the combined rate.","why":"First printer: 1,800 / 45 = 40 pages per minute. Second: 1,800 / 30 = 60 pages per minute. Together: 100 pages per minute. 3,000 pages take 3,000 / 100 = 30 minutes. Averaging the times (37.5) is a trap.","diag":{"2":"Averaging 45 and 30 does not combine two machines; add their rates."}},{"id":"aw-015","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"work","diff":4,"stem":"Pipe A can fill an empty tank in 6 hours and pipe B can fill it in 9 hours, while drain C can empty a full tank in 12 hours. If the tank is empty and all three are opened at once, how many hours will it take to fill the tank, to the nearest hundredth?","opts":["2.77","3.27","3.6","4.5","5.14"],"correct":4,"move":"Fillers add, drains subtract, then invert the net rate.","why":"Rates: A = 1/6, B = 1/9, C = -1/12 tank per hour. Common denominator 36: 6/36 + 4/36 - 3/36 = 7/36. Time = 36/7 = 5.142..., about 5.14 hours. Treating the drain as a filler gives 36/13, about 2.77; A and B alone give 3.6.","diag":{"0":"The drain removes water. Subtract 1/12 instead of adding it.","2":"That is A and B without the drain."}},{"id":"aw-016","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":3,"stem":"A technician has 50 liters of a 12% sugar solution. How many liters of water must be evaporated so that the remaining solution is 20% sugar?","opts":["8","10","20","24","30"],"correct":2,"move":"Evaporation removes water only; hold the sugar fixed and solve for the new total.","why":"Sugar: 12% of 50 = 6 liters, and it stays 6 liters. For 6 liters to be 20% of the solution, the total must be 6 / 0.20 = 30 liters. So 50 - 30 = 20 liters of water must evaporate. 30 is the remaining volume, not the amount removed.","diag":{"0":"Subtracting percentages (20 - 12 = 8) does not track the sugar.","4":"That is the volume left after evaporating. The question asks how much water leaves."}},{"id":"aw-017","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":3,"stem":"A 30-kilogram metal alloy is 20% copper by weight. How many kilograms of pure copper must be melted into it so that the new alloy is 40% copper?","opts":["10","12","15","18","20"],"correct":0,"move":"Adding a pure ingredient raises both the part and the whole; set (part + x) / (whole + x) equal to the target.","why":"Copper now: 20% of 30 = 6 kg. Add x kg of copper: (6 + x) / (30 + x) = 0.40. Then 6 + x = 12 + 0.4x, so 0.6x = 6 and x = 10 kg. Check: 16 kg of copper in 40 kg is 40%.","diag":{"1":"Doubling the copper from 6 to 12 does not double the percent, because the total grows to 36 kg."}},{"id":"aw-018","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":4,"stem":"A coffee shop blends beans that cost 8 dollars per pound with beans that cost 14 dollars per pound to make 60 pounds of a blend that costs 10 dollars per pound. How many pounds of the 8-dollar beans are in the blend?","opts":["20","24","30","36","40"],"correct":4,"move":"Weighted average: the blend sits closer to the ingredient you use more of.","why":"Let x be the pounds of 8-dollar beans; the rest, 60 - x, are 14-dollar beans. Cost: 8x + 14(60 - x) = 10(60). So 840 - 6x = 600, 6x = 240, and x = 40 pounds. Distance check: 10 is 2 above 8 and 4 below 14, so the cheaper beans get twice the weight, 40 to 20.","diag":{"0":"That is the amount of 14-dollar beans. The blend price is closer to 8, so the cheap beans dominate.","2":"An even split would cost 11 dollars per pound, not 10."}},{"id":"aw-019","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":3,"stem":"Solution P is 25% alcohol and solution Q is 60% alcohol. In what ratio, by volume, should P and Q be mixed (P to Q) to produce a solution that is 40% alcohol?","opts":["1/2","3/5","3/4","1","4/3"],"correct":4,"move":"Mixing ratio = (distance of the other ingredient from the target) : (distance of this one).","why":"Let the mix have p liters of P and q liters of Q: 0.25p + 0.60q = 0.40(p + q). So 0.20q = 0.15p, and p / q = 0.20 / 0.15 = 4/3. Shortcut: Q is 20 points from 40 and P is 15 points from 40, so P : Q = 20 : 15 = 4 : 3. More of P is needed because P is closer to the target.","diag":{"2":"That is the ratio flipped. The ingredient closer to the target gets the larger share, and P (15 away) is closer than Q (20 away)."}},{"id":"aw-020","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"mixture","diff":4,"stem":"A 40-liter tank holds a 30% acid solution. Some of the solution is drained and replaced with the same volume of pure acid, and the result is a 45% acid solution. How many liters were drained, to the nearest tenth?","opts":["8.6","9.2","10.0","12.0","15.0"],"correct":0,"move":"Drain-and-replace: acid lost is 30% of x, acid gained is 100% of x; the volume stays 40.","why":"Acid at the start: 0.30 × 40 = 12 liters. Draining x liters removes 0.3x liters of acid; adding x liters of pure acid adds x. New acid: 12 + 0.7x, still in 40 liters. Set 12 + 0.7x = 0.45 × 40 = 18, so 0.7x = 6 and x = 60/7, about 8.6 liters. Forgetting that the drained liquid carries acid away gives 12 + x = 18, or x = 6, which is too small.","diag":{"2":"Draining removes acid as well as water: each liter drained takes 0.3 liters of acid with it."}},{"id":"aw-021","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":2,"stem":"The price of a textbook rises by 25%. Later the price is cut so that it returns to exactly its original value. By what percent was the higher price cut?","opts":["15%","20%","25%","30%","33.3%"],"correct":1,"move":"Undoing a percent change uses the new price as the base: divide by the multiplier.","why":"Say the price was 100. After a 25% rise it is 125. To get back to 100, cut 25 from 125: 25 / 125 = 20%. The cut is a smaller percent than the rise because it is taken from a larger base.","diag":{"2":"A 25% cut from 125 would leave 93.75, not 100. The base is now 125."}},{"id":"aw-022","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":4,"stem":"A town's population increased by 20% in one year and then decreased by 15% the following year, ending at 30,600. What was the population at the start of the two years?","opts":["28,800","30,000","31,212","32,000","36,000"],"correct":1,"move":"Work backward by dividing by the combined multiplier: 1.20 × 0.85 = 1.02.","why":"Two changes multiply: start × 1.20 × 0.85 = start × 1.02. So start = 30,600 / 1.02 = 30,000. Check: 30,000 rises to 36,000, then falls to 30,600. Multiplying by 1.02 instead of dividing gives 31,212.","diag":{"2":"You applied the changes forward to the ending number. Divide by 1.02 to go back.","4":"That is the population after the first year, not the start."}},{"id":"aw-023","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"In a study, 40% of the mice are placed in group X and the rest in group Y. In group X, 25% of the mice show a response, and in group Y, 50% show a response. What percent of all the mice show a response?","opts":["25%","30%","37.5%","40%","75%"],"correct":3,"move":"Percent of a percent: weight each group's rate by the group's share.","why":"Responders from X: 25% of 40% = 10% of all mice. Responders from Y: 50% of 60% = 30% of all mice. Total: 10% + 30% = 40%. Averaging 25% and 50% gives 37.5%, which ignores that Y is the bigger group.","diag":{"2":"The groups are not the same size; Y holds 60% of the mice, so its rate counts more."}},{"id":"aw-024","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":4,"stem":"A store marks up the cost of a lamp by 40% to set its list price, then sells it at 15% off the list price. A customer also uses a coupon for 10 dollars off the sale price and pays 109 dollars. What did the lamp cost the store?","opts":["80 dollars","84 dollars","91.6 dollars","95 dollars","100 dollars"],"correct":4,"move":"Undo the steps in reverse order: add back the coupon first, then divide by the percent multipliers.","why":"Before the coupon the sale price was 109 + 10 = 119 dollars. The markup and discount multiply: 1.40 × 0.85 = 1.19. So cost = 119 / 1.19 = 100 dollars. Dividing 109 by 1.19 (forgetting the coupon) gives about 91.6.","diag":{"1":"Plug it back in: 84 × 1.4 × 0.85 = 99.96, and after the 10-dollar coupon the customer would pay about 90, not 109.","2":"Add the 10-dollar coupon back first: the sale price was 119."}},{"id":"aw-025","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"percent","diff":3,"stem":"A student's score rose from 72 on the first practice test to 90 on the second. The third score was 10% lower than the second. By what percent is the third score higher than the first?","opts":["12.5%","15%","22.5%","25%","35%"],"correct":0,"move":"Get the actual third score first, then compare it to the first score.","why":"Third score: 90 × 0.90 = 81. Change from the first: 81 - 72 = 9. Percent of the first: 9 / 72 = 12.5%. Subtracting percents (+25% then -10%) to get 15% does not work, because the 10% is taken from 90, not 72.","diag":{"1":"The 25% rise and the 10% drop use different bases; multiply 1.25 × 0.9 = 1.125.","3":"That is the rise from the first test to the second only."}},{"id":"aw-026","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"interest","diff":3,"stem":"A student deposits 5,000 dollars in an account that pays 4% interest per year, compounded annually. If no other deposits or withdrawals are made, how much interest will the account earn in 3 years?","opts":["600 dollars","624.32 dollars","630 dollars","648.92 dollars","5,624.32 dollars"],"correct":1,"move":"Compound growth: multiply by (1 + rate) once per year, then subtract the starting amount for interest only.","why":"After 3 years: 5,000 × 1.04³ = 5,000 × 1.124864 = 5,624.32 dollars. Interest earned: 5,624.32 - 5,000 = 624.32 dollars. Simple interest would be 600, and 5,624.32 is the full account value, not the interest.","diag":{"0":"That is simple interest. Compounding earns interest on earlier interest too.","4":"That is the account total. Subtract the 5,000 dollar deposit."}},{"id":"aw-027","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"interest","diff":3,"stem":"A bacterial population grows by 50% every hour. If a sample starts with 800 bacteria, how many bacteria will it contain after 4 hours?","opts":["2,400","3,200","3,600","4,050","6,075"],"correct":3,"move":"Growth by a fixed percent multiplies each step: 800 × 1.5^4.","why":"Each hour multiplies by 1.5: 800, then 1,200, 1,800, 2,700, and 4,050. Adding 400 each hour (50% of the starting amount) gives the straight-line 2,400, which misses the compounding.","diag":{"0":"The 50% is of the current amount, not the original 800, so the hourly increase grows.","4":"That is 5 hours of growth."}},{"id":"aw-028","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"interest","diff":3,"stem":"How much more interest does 2,000 dollars earn in 2 years at 10% per year compounded annually than at 10% per year simple interest?","opts":["20 dollars","22 dollars","40 dollars","200 dollars","420 dollars"],"correct":0,"move":"The compound-simple gap in year 2 is the interest on the first year's interest.","why":"Simple: 2,000 × 0.10 × 2 = 400 dollars. Compound: 2,000 × 1.1² = 2,420, so 420 dollars of interest. Difference: 20 dollars, which is exactly 10% of the first year's 200 dollars of interest.","diag":{"4":"That is the compound interest itself. Subtract the 400 dollars of simple interest."}},{"id":"aw-029","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"interest","diff":4,"stem":"The amount of a medication in a patient's body decreases by 20% every hour. A patient takes a 250-milligram dose. How many milligrams remain after 3 hours?","opts":["50","100","102.4","120","128"],"correct":4,"move":"Decay by a percent keeps (1 - rate) each step: multiply by 0.8 per hour.","why":"Each hour keeps 80%: 250 × 0.8 = 200, then 160, then 128 milligrams. Taking 20% of 250 off three times (60% total) gives 100, which overstates the loss because each later 20% comes from a smaller amount.","diag":{"1":"Each hour removes 20% of what is left, not 20% of the original dose.","2":"That is after 4 hours."}},{"id":"aw-030","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"interest","diff":2,"stem":"An account pays 6% simple interest per year on the original deposit. How many years will it take for a deposit of 4,000 dollars to grow to 5,200 dollars?","opts":["2","3","4","5","30"],"correct":3,"move":"Simple interest earns the same dollar amount every year: divide the total interest by one year's interest.","why":"Interest needed: 5,200 - 4,000 = 1,200 dollars. One year earns 6% of 4,000 = 240 dollars. Years: 1,200 / 240 = 5.","diag":{"4":"That divides 1,200 by 40. One year of interest is 6% of 4,000, which is 240."}},{"id":"aw-031","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"Maria is now 3 times as old as her son. In 12 years, she will be twice as old as her son will be then. How old is Maria now?","opts":["36","39","42","45","48"],"correct":0,"move":"Ages: write both people now, add the same years to both, then set up the later relationship.","why":"Let the son be s; Maria is 3s. In 12 years: 3s + 12 = 2(s + 12) = 2s + 24, so s = 12 and Maria is 36. Check: in 12 years they are 48 and 24, and 48 is twice 24.","diag":{"4":"That is Maria's age in 12 years."}},{"id":"aw-032","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"ratios","diff":3,"stem":"In a class, the ratio of boys to girls is 3 : 4. After 6 more boys join the class and no one leaves, the ratio of boys to girls becomes 1 : 1. How many students are in the class now?","opts":["24","30","36","42","48"],"correct":4,"move":"Write the ratio with a multiplier (3k and 4k), apply the change, then solve for k.","why":"Boys 3k, girls 4k. After 6 boys join: 3k + 6 = 4k, so k = 6. Originally 18 boys and 24 girls (42 students). Now 24 boys and 24 girls, so 48 students. 42 is the class before the new boys arrived.","diag":{"3":"That is the class before the 6 boys joined. The question asks about now."}},{"id":"aw-033","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"ratios","diff":4,"stem":"Three partners share a prize in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5. The partner with the largest share receives 1,200 dollars more than the partner with the smallest share. How much does the middle partner receive?","opts":["600 dollars","800 dollars","1,000 dollars","1,200 dollars","2,000 dollars"],"correct":3,"move":"A difference between shares equals the difference in parts: find the value of one part.","why":"The largest and smallest shares differ by 5 - 2 = 3 parts, and that is 1,200 dollars, so one part is 400 dollars. The middle partner has 3 parts: 3 × 400 = 1,200 dollars. The whole prize is 10 parts, or 4,000 dollars.","diag":{"1":"That is the smallest share (2 parts). The middle partner has 3 parts.","4":"That is the largest share (5 parts)."}},{"id":"aw-034","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"ratios","diff":2,"stem":"Eight identical pumps working together can drain a pool in 15 hours. At the same rate per pump, how many hours would 12 of these pumps take to drain the pool?","opts":["10","12","18","20","22.5"],"correct":0,"move":"More workers, less time: workers × time stays constant (inverse proportion).","why":"The job is 8 × 15 = 120 pump-hours. With 12 pumps: 120 / 12 = 10 hours. Setting up a direct proportion (8 is to 15 as 12 is to x) gives 22.5, which wrongly makes more pumps take longer.","diag":{"4":"More pumps finish faster. Pump-hours stay fixed at 120, so divide by 12."}},{"id":"aw-035","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A committee of 3 students is chosen at random from a group of 5 biology majors and 4 chemistry majors. What is the probability that the committee contains exactly 2 biology majors?","opts":["5/42","20/63","10/21","5/9","2/3"],"correct":2,"move":"Count favorable groups by choosing from each pool, then divide by all possible groups.","why":"All committees: C(9,3) = 84. Exactly 2 biology and 1 chemistry: C(5,2) × C(4,1) = 10 × 4 = 40. Probability: 40 / 84 = 10/21. Forgetting to choose the chemistry member gives 10/84 = 5/42.","diag":{"0":"That counts only the 10 biology pairs. Each pair can go with any of the 4 chemistry majors, so there are 40 favorable committees."}},{"id":"aw-036","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"counting","diff":3,"stem":"A lab access code consists of 2 letters followed by 3 digits. The letters are chosen from the 26 letters of the alphabet and may not repeat, while the digits (0 through 9) may repeat. How many different codes are possible?","opts":["468,000","585,000","650,000","676,000","1,000,000"],"correct":2,"move":"Fill slots left to right and multiply the choices; a no-repeat slot loses one option.","why":"Letters: 26 choices, then 25 (no repeat). Digits: 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. Total: 26 × 25 × 1,000 = 650,000. Letting the letters repeat gives 676,000; forbidding repeated digits gives 26 × 25 × 720 = 468,000.","diag":{"0":"The digits may repeat, so each digit has 10 choices.","3":"The letters may not repeat, so the second letter has 25 choices."}},{"id":"aw-037","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"probability","diff":4,"stem":"Three fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that at least one of the dice shows a 6?","opts":["91/216","1/2","125/216","2/3","5/6"],"correct":0,"move":"\"At least one\": compute the chance of none, then subtract from 1.","why":"No 6 on one die: 5/6. No 6 on all three: (5/6)³ = 125/216. At least one 6: 1 - 125/216 = 91/216, about 42%. Adding 1/6 three times gives 1/2, which double-counts rolls with two or three 6s.","diag":{"1":"Adding 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 counts rolls with more than one 6 more than once.","2":"That is the probability of no 6 at all, the complement."}},{"id":"aw-038","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"probability","diff":3,"stem":"A bag contains 3 red, 5 green, and 2 blue chips. Two chips are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both chips are the same color?","opts":["1/4","14/45","1/3","19/50","7/15"],"correct":1,"move":"Without replacement, count same-color pairs with C(n,2) for each color over C(total,2).","why":"All pairs: C(10,2) = 45. Same color: red C(3,2) = 3, green C(5,2) = 10, blue C(2,2) = 1, total 14. Probability: 14/45. Treating the draws as with replacement gives 0.09 + 0.25 + 0.04 = 0.38 = 19/50.","diag":{"3":"That treats the draws as independent with replacement. After one chip is drawn, the bag has one fewer of that color."}},{"id":"aw-039","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"statistics","diff":3,"stem":"The mean of a student's 8 quiz scores is 82. When the lowest score is dropped, the mean of the remaining 7 scores is 85. What was the lowest score?","opts":["55","58","61","64","79"],"correct":2,"move":"Turn every mean into a total; the removed value is the difference of totals.","why":"Total of 8 scores: 8 × 82 = 656. Total of 7 scores: 7 × 85 = 595. Dropped score: 656 - 595 = 61. A quick subtraction of means (85 - 82 = 3) tells you nothing directly; the totals do.","diag":{"4":"That subtracts the mean change from 82. Work with totals: 656 - 595."}},{"id":"aw-040","format":"aw","skill":"applied-wp","topic":"statistics","diff":4,"stem":"A course grade is weighted 30% homework, 30% midterm, and 40% final exam. A student has a homework average of 90 and a midterm score of 60. What final exam score does the student need to earn an overall grade of exactly 80?","opts":["80","82.5","87.5","90","92.5"],"correct":2,"move":"Weighted average: sum of (weight × score) equals the target, then solve for the one unknown.","why":"Points so far: 0.30 × 90 + 0.30 × 60 = 27 + 18 = 45. Needed overall: 80. The final must supply 80 - 45 = 35 weighted points, so 0.40 × final = 35 and the final = 87.5. Check: 27 + 18 + 0.40 × 87.5 = 27 + 18 + 35 = 80.","diag":{"0":"That is the target grade itself. The final has to make up for the 60 on the midterm.","3":"That treats the three parts as equally weighted: (90 + 60 + x) / 3 = 80 gives 90. The final counts 40%, so it needs less."}}]});
